window.SAFETY_DATA = {"generated": "2026-10-08", "divisions": [{"code": "electric", "name": "Electric Services"}, {"code": "environmental", "name": "Environmental Services"}, {"code": "public", "name": "Public Services"}], "courses": [{"slug": "fall-protection-general", "title": "Fall Protection: General Requirements", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "standard_title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 1, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Falls are the leading cause of death in construction, and fall protection is the most frequently cited OSHA standard year after year. This course explains when fall protection is required on public works jobs, what hazards trigger it, how guardrails, safety nets and personal fall arrest systems work, and what has to be in place before anyone ties off. It applies to every division because every division has crews working on edges, roofs, tanks, structures and equipment.", "objectives": ["Identify the height triggers for fall protection in construction work (6 feet) and general industry work (4 feet) and tell which one applies to a task.", "Recognize the fall hazards named in 1926.501 that show up on public works jobs, including open edges, leading edges, holes, excavations, roofs, tanks and structures.", "Describe the three main fall protection systems and explain why guardrails are the first choice.", "List the components of a personal fall arrest system and state the limits on anchorage strength, free fall distance and arresting force.", "Inspect fall arrest equipment before each use and know when a component must be removed from service.", "Explain swing fall and why a rescue plan must be in place before anyone ties off."], "sections": [{"heading": "When fall protection is required", "html": "<p>OSHA's construction standard, <strong>29 CFR 1926.501</strong>, requires fall protection whenever an employee works on a surface with an unprotected side or edge <strong>6 feet or more</strong> above a lower level. The employer must first confirm the surface can hold the workers, then provide a guardrail system, a safety net system or a personal fall arrest system (PFAS).</p>\n<p>Public works crews also do <strong>general industry</strong> work covered by <strong>29 CFR 1910.28</strong> (walking-working surfaces), which uses a <strong>4 foot</strong> trigger. The task decides which rule applies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Building, altering, repairing or demolishing a structure, such as replacing a culvert headwall or re-roofing a pump house, is construction. The 6 foot trigger applies.</li>\n<li>Routine maintenance and operations, such as inspecting a clarifier walkway or changing a light on the shop mezzanine, is general industry. The 4 foot trigger applies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When in doubt, use the stricter 4 foot trigger. Above <strong>dangerous equipment</strong>, such as an open tank or the auger in a salt spreader, guards are required at any height.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Fall protection has held the top spot on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards for more than a decade. Inspectors cite 1926.501 so often because the hazard is easy to see and easy to prove. A worker on an edge with no guardrail and no harness is a citation on sight.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(b)(13) Residential construction.</strong> Workers on roofs and floor decks with no protection at all.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(1) Unprotected sides and edges.</strong> Open edges of floors, decks, platforms and loading areas.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(10) and (b)(11) Roofing work.</strong> Low-slope and steep roofs with no system in place.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(4) Holes.</strong> Uncovered floor holes and unguarded skylights.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common thread is not missing equipment. Most crews own harnesses and guardrail parts. The gear stayed in the truck because the job was 'only going to take a minute.' Short tasks are where most of our exposure is, and where the citations come from.</p>"}, {"heading": "Fall hazards you will meet in public works", "html": "<p>These are the situations in the standard that show up on municipal jobs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unprotected sides and edges.</strong> Any open edge 6 feet or more above the next level: a bridge deck under repair, a retaining wall top or a lift station roof.</li>\n<li><strong>Leading edges.</strong> An edge that moves as work advances, such as deck forms on a culvert or bridge. If guardrails, nets and PFAS are truly infeasible, a written fall protection plan under 1926.502(k) is required. Inconvenient does not mean infeasible.</li>\n<li><strong>Holes and skylights.</strong> Any hole more than 6 feet above a lower level needs a <strong>cover</strong>, a guardrail or PFAS. Covers must hold twice the expected load, be secured so they cannot slide, and be marked HOLE or COVER. A skylight, an open tank or vault hatch, and the gap left when a grate is pulled are all holes.</li>\n<li><strong>Excavations.</strong> Edges 6 feet or more deep need guardrails, fences or barricades when the edge is hidden from view. Wells, pits and shafts that deep need guardrails, barricades or covers.</li>\n<li><strong>Roofs.</strong> Low-slope roofs allow guardrails, nets, PFAS, or a warning line combined with one of those. Steep roofs require guardrails with toeboards, nets or PFAS.</li>\n<li><strong>Bridges, structures and tanks.</strong> Water tower catwalks, clarifier bridges and digester tops have fixed railings for a reason. When a railing comes off for a repair, replacement protection goes in first.</li>\n<li><strong>Poles and bucket trucks.</strong> Pole work and aerial lift work have their own rules under 29 CFR 1910.269, 1926 Subpart V and 1926.453. Tie off in the bucket every time, and use fall protection whenever you climb or change position on a pole.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "The three main systems", "html": "<p>The standard names three conventional systems.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Guardrail systems</strong> are the first choice because they protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker. The top rail sits <strong>42 inches, plus or minus 3 inches</strong>, above the walking surface and must withstand a 200 pound force. A midrail goes about halfway up. Add toeboards when people work below.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety net systems</strong> catch a worker after a fall. Nets go as close as practical under the work surface and never more than 30 feet below it. They are rare in public works.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal fall arrest systems</strong> stop a fall that has already started. They depend on correct equipment, a sound anchor and a trained worker, and fit short tasks where a guardrail cannot be built.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>warning line</strong> keeps workers back from the edge on low-slope roofs, and a <strong>controlled access zone</strong> limits who can enter an area where conventional protection is not in use. Both have strict conditions and are used only under a competent person.</p>\n<p>Remember the order of preference. First, remove the exposure by working from the ground or a lift platform with rails. Second, guard the edge. Third, rely on personal equipment.</p>"}, {"heading": "Personal fall arrest systems: components and limits", "html": "<p>A PFAS has three parts, often called the ABCs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A, Anchorage.</strong> The anchor must hold at least <strong>5,000 pounds per worker</strong> attached, or be designed, installed and used under the supervision of a <strong>qualified person</strong> as part of a complete system with a safety factor of at least two. A guardrail, a conduit run or a vent pipe is not an anchor.</li>\n<li><strong>B, Body support.</strong> Only a <strong>full body harness</strong> is allowed. Body belts are not accepted for fall arrest.</li>\n<li><strong>C, Connectors.</strong> A <strong>lanyard</strong> or self-retracting lifeline connects the harness to the anchor. Lanyards need a 5,000 pound breaking strength, and snaphooks must be self-locking. A <strong>deceleration device</strong>, such as a shock-absorbing lanyard or self-retracting lifeline, limits the force on your body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The system as a whole must meet these limits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Free fall of no more than 6 feet</strong>, and the worker must not contact a lower level.</li>\n<li>Maximum arresting force of <strong>1,800 pounds</strong> with a full body harness.</li>\n<li>Deceleration distance of <strong>no more than 3.5 feet</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Add it up before you tie off. A 6 foot lanyard, 3.5 feet of deceleration, the worker's height below the D-ring and a safety margin can mean more than 17 feet of clearance below the anchor. If the next level is closer, use a self-retracting lifeline anchored overhead or a different system.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection, swing fall and rescue", "html": "<p><strong>Inspect before each use.</strong> Check webbing for cuts, burns, frays, chemical damage and sun fading. Check stitching, D-rings, buckles and snaphook gates. Check the shock pack on a lanyard for a deployed indicator and a self-retracting lifeline for smooth retraction and a working brake. Any system that has caught a fall comes out of service until a competent person inspects it and clears it as undamaged. Anything defective gets tagged and removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Swing fall.</strong> If you anchor off to the side and fall, you swing like a pendulum back under the anchor. You can hit a wall or structure with full force, and the swing adds fall distance. Anchor as close to directly overhead as you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Rescue.</strong> The employer must provide for <strong>prompt rescue</strong> or make sure workers can rescue themselves. Hanging in a harness restricts blood flow in the legs, and a person can pass out in a short time. Before anyone ties off, the crew must know how a fallen worker will get down: a bucket truck on site, a rescue kit with a descent device, or a ladder that reaches the worker. Calling 911 and waiting is not a rescue plan. Never work tied off alone.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Substation steel, transformer tops and capacitor racks put technicians above 4 feet during maintenance and above 6 feet on construction projects. In a bucket, attach the lanyard to the anchor on the boom or basket, never to the bucket lip. Meter technicians on commercial rooftops face unguarded edges and skylights; treat them like any other roof.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants are full of open tanks, hatches, clarifier bridges and digester roofs, and a fall into a tank is also a drowning hazard. Pulling a grate or railing to lift a pump creates a hole; cover it or guard it right away. Solid waste crews face fall hazards at the transfer station tipping floor and on top of roll-off containers.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Bridge deck repairs, culvert headwalls and retaining walls are construction work with the 6 foot trigger. Signal technicians tie off in the bucket every time. Facilities staff on flat roofs need a warning line, guardrail or PFAS. Forestry crews in aerial lifts tie off, and climbing arborists follow ANSI Z133. Fleet mechanics on top of plow trucks and tanker bodies are above 4 feet; use the shop's fall arrest rail or a platform with rails.</p>\n<p>In every division, ask the same question: what will stop me if I slip right now? If the answer is nothing, stop and set up protection.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Construction work needs fall protection at 6 feet, general industry work needs it at 4 feet, and public works crews do both kinds of work.", "Guardrails are the first choice because they protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker.", "A hole is anything you can fall through, including skylights, open hatches and the gap left when a grate is pulled, and it needs a cover, a guardrail or PFAS.", "Anchor points must hold 5,000 pounds per worker or be engineered by a qualified person with a safety factor of two.", "Only a full body harness is allowed in a fall arrest system, free fall is limited to 6 feet, and the worker must never contact a lower level.", "Inspect every component before each use and remove anything that has caught a fall or shows damage.", "Anchor overhead to limit swing fall, and have a rescue plan in place before anyone ties off."], "questions": [{"text": "Under the construction standard 1926.501, at what height above a lower level is fall protection required at an unprotected edge?", "options": ["4 feet", "10 feet", "6 feet", "8 feet"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The construction trigger in 1926.501 is 6 feet. The 4 foot figure is the general industry trigger under 1910.28."}, {"text": "A facilities worker is doing routine maintenance on a shop mezzanine with an open edge. Which height trigger applies?", "options": ["4 feet, because routine maintenance is general industry work under 1910.28", "6 feet, because all public works field work is construction", "10 feet, because mezzanines are storage areas", "No trigger applies to indoor work"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Routine maintenance and operations are general industry work, so the 4 foot trigger in 1910.28 applies. The 6 foot trigger is for construction tasks such as building, altering or repairing a structure."}, {"text": "According to the course, what is the most common reason crews get cited under 1926.501?", "options": ["They do not own guardrail components", "Harnesses are too expensive for small departments", "Inspectors cannot tell construction from maintenance", "Equipment stays in the truck for tasks that will 'only take a minute'"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Most crews own the equipment. Citations happen when it is not used, usually on short tasks that the crew expected to finish quickly."}, {"text": "Which statement about hole covers is correct?", "options": ["Covers only need to hold the weight of one worker", "Covers must hold twice the expected load, be secured against sliding, and be marked HOLE or COVER", "A piece of plywood laid over the opening is enough if everyone on the crew knows it is there", "Skylights do not count as holes because they have glass or plastic panels"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Covers must support at least twice the load that could be placed on them, be secured so they cannot be displaced, and be marked so people know what they are. A skylight is treated as a hole."}, {"text": "Why are guardrail systems the preferred fall protection system?", "options": ["They protect everyone in the area without any action by the worker", "They are the cheapest system to buy", "They are the only system allowed on roofs", "They do not require a competent person"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A guardrail is a passive system. It works for every person who walks near the edge, whether or not they remembered to clip in."}, {"text": "What strength must a fall arrest anchorage have if it has not been engineered by a qualified person?", "options": ["1,800 pounds per worker", "3,000 pounds per worker", "5,000 pounds per worker", "10,000 pounds total"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An anchorage must support at least 5,000 pounds per worker attached, unless it is designed and used under a qualified person as part of a system with a safety factor of at least two. The 1,800 pound figure is the limit on arresting force."}, {"text": "What is the maximum free fall distance allowed for a personal fall arrest system?", "options": ["3.5 feet", "6 feet", "10 feet", "12 feet"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A PFAS must be rigged so the worker cannot free fall more than 6 feet or contact a lower level. The 3.5 foot figure is the maximum deceleration distance, not the free fall limit."}, {"text": "What should happen to a lanyard that has arrested a fall?", "options": ["Return it to service if the webbing looks fine", "Keep using it until the shock pack is fully deployed", "Use it only for positioning from now on", "Remove it from service until a competent person inspects it and determines it is undamaged"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Any PFAS component subjected to impact loading must be removed from service immediately and may not be used again unless a competent person inspects it and finds it undamaged."}, {"text": "What is the best way to reduce the swing fall hazard?", "options": ["Use a longer lanyard so the swing is slower", "Tie off to a guardrail near the edge", "Anchor as close to directly overhead as possible", "Wear a body belt instead of a harness"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Swing fall happens when the anchor is off to the side. Anchoring directly overhead keeps the worker from swinging into walls or structure and keeps the fall distance shorter."}, {"text": "Which of these meets the standard's requirement to provide for prompt rescue?", "options": ["Calling 911 after a fall and waiting for help", "Having a bucket truck, rescue kit or ladder on site with a crew that knows how to use it before anyone ties off", "Instructing the fallen worker to unclip and climb down", "Working alone so no one else is exposed"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The employer must provide for prompt rescue or make sure workers can rescue themselves. That means the method and equipment are in place and the crew knows the plan before the work starts."}]}, {"slug": "hazard-communication", "title": "Hazard Communication (HazCom)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "standard_title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 2, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Hazard Communication, 29 CFR 1910.1200, gives every employee the right to know the hazards of the chemicals they work with and how to protect themselves. This course covers the written program, chemical inventory, Safety Data Sheets, GHS labels and pictograms, secondary container labeling and training requirements, with examples drawn from water and wastewater plants, fleet shops, streets, forestry and electric operations. It also explains the gaps that most often lead to HazCom citations and how crews can close them.", "objectives": ["Explain the employee right to know and the five working parts of a hazard communication program.", "Locate and read the key sections of a Safety Data Sheet for hazard, first aid, handling and PPE information.", "Identify the six elements of a GHS label and the meaning of each of the nine pictograms.", "Apply workplace labeling rules to secondary and portable containers.", "Recognize the hazardous chemicals common to your division and where to find their SDSs.", "Describe when HazCom training is required and the most common reasons for citations."], "sections": [{"heading": "Your right to know", "html": "<p>The Hazard Communication standard, often called <strong>HazCom</strong> or the <strong>Right to Know</strong> rule, is built on a simple idea: you have the right to know what chemicals you work with, what harm they can cause, and how to protect yourself. Manufacturers and importers must classify the hazards of their products and pass that information along through <strong>labels</strong> and <strong>Safety Data Sheets (SDS)</strong>. Your employer must then pass it on to you. HazCom covers any hazardous chemical you could be exposed to at work or in a foreseeable emergency.</p>\n<p>The standard has five working parts:<br />\n- A written hazard communication program<br />\n- A chemical inventory<br />\n- Labels and other warnings<br />\n- Safety Data Sheets<br />\n- Employee training</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>HazCom sits near the top of OSHA's most frequently cited standards list year after year. The rule is simple. The paperwork and daily habits it requires slip when crews are busy.</p>\n<p>The citations inspectors write most often fall into four groups:<br />\n- <strong>No written program.</strong> The department cannot produce one, or it has a generic template that never says who is responsible locally.<br />\n- <strong>No SDS available.</strong> A chemical is in use but its SDS cannot be found, or it is locked in an office the night shift cannot reach.<br />\n- <strong>No training, or no record of it.</strong> Employees cannot say what a pictogram means or where the SDS file is.<br />\n- <strong>Unlabeled containers.</strong> Spray bottles, parts washer tanks, gas cans and brine totes with no label, a faded label or one that just says cleaner.</p>\n<p>Every one of these is preventable with a working program and regular attention.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written program, chemical inventory and training", "html": "<p>Employers with hazardous chemicals in the workplace must have a <strong>written hazard communication program</strong>. It describes how the department handles labels, SDSs and training. It must include a <strong>list of the hazardous chemicals</strong> known to be present, using the same product identifier that appears on the label and the SDS so all three match. It must also explain how employees learn about the hazards of <strong>non-routine tasks</strong>, such as cleaning a clarifier, and about chemicals in <strong>unlabeled pipes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Keep the inventory current. A new product goes on the list, with its SDS on file, before it is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Training</strong> is required:<br />\n- At the time of <strong>initial assignment</strong> to a job where hazardous chemicals are present.<br />\n- Whenever a <strong>new chemical hazard</strong> the employee has not been trained on is introduced into the work area.</p>\n<p>Training covers where chemicals are used, where the program, chemical list and SDSs are kept, how to detect a release, the hazards, the protective measures, and how to read labels and SDSs. Keep training records.</p>"}, {"heading": "Safety Data Sheets", "html": "<p>A <strong>Safety Data Sheet (SDS)</strong> is the detailed reference for a chemical product. Every SDS follows the same <strong>16-section format</strong>, so once you learn where to look you can find what you need on any sheet:<br />\n- Section 1: Identification<br />\n- Section 2: Hazard(s) identification<br />\n- Section 3: Composition and ingredients<br />\n- Section 4: First-aid measures<br />\n- Section 5: Fire-fighting measures<br />\n- Section 6: Accidental release measures<br />\n- Section 7: Handling and storage<br />\n- Section 8: Exposure controls and personal protection<br />\n- Section 9: Physical and chemical properties<br />\n- Section 10: Stability and reactivity<br />\n- Section 11: Toxicological information<br />\n- Section 12: Ecological information<br />\n- Section 13: Disposal considerations<br />\n- Section 14: Transport information<br />\n- Section 15: Regulatory information<br />\n- Section 16: Other information</p>\n<p>For daily work, four sections matter most. <strong>Section 2</strong> gives the hazard classification, signal word, pictograms and hazard statements. <strong>Section 4</strong> tells you what to do right away if someone is splashed, breathes vapors or swallows the product. <strong>Section 7</strong> covers handling and storage. <strong>Section 8</strong> lists exposure limits and the gloves, eye protection and respirator you need. Section 10 lists incompatible materials.</p>\n<p>SDSs must be <strong>readily accessible during each work shift</strong> to employees in their work areas. A shop binder, a control room computer or a phone app all work, as long as everyone can reach the sheet without delay or permission. Electronic systems need a backup for outages. If you cannot find an SDS, tell your supervisor.</p>"}, {"heading": "Reading GHS labels", "html": "<p>HazCom uses the <strong>Globally Harmonized System (GHS)</strong> so labels look the same no matter who made the product. Every shipped container must carry a label with six elements:<br />\n- <strong>Product identifier.</strong> The name or code that matches the SDS and the inventory.<br />\n- <strong>Signal word.</strong> <strong>Danger</strong> for the more severe hazards, <strong>Warning</strong> for the less severe. A label carries only one.<br />\n- <strong>Hazard statements.</strong> Standard phrases that describe the hazard, such as \"Causes severe skin burns and eye damage.\"<br />\n- <strong>Precautionary statements.</strong> Standard phrases on prevention, response, storage and disposal.<br />\n- <strong>Pictograms.</strong> Black symbols on a white background inside a red diamond.<br />\n- <strong>Supplier information.</strong> Name, address and phone number of the manufacturer or importer.</p>\n<p>Read the label before you open a container for the first time, and again if you have not used the product in a while. Never remove or deface a supplier label.</p>\n<p>One exception: pesticides and herbicides are labeled under EPA rules, so their labels look different. They are still covered by HazCom for SDSs, the inventory and training.</p>"}, {"heading": "The nine pictograms", "html": "<p>Pictograms give you the hazard at a glance. Learn all nine:<br />\n- <strong>Health Hazard</strong> (silhouette with a starburst on the chest): carcinogens, reproductive toxins, respiratory sensitizers, target organ toxicity.<br />\n- <strong>Flame</strong>: flammable liquids, gases and solids. Gasoline, acetylene, many aerosols.<br />\n- <strong>Exclamation Mark</strong>: irritants, skin sensitizers, products harmful if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed. Many cleaners and deicers.<br />\n- <strong>Gas Cylinder</strong>: gases under pressure. Chlorine, oxygen, SF6, welding gases.<br />\n- <strong>Corrosion</strong>: skin burns and eye damage, or corrosive to metals. Sodium hypochlorite, battery acid, caustic soda.<br />\n- <strong>Exploding Bomb</strong>: explosives, self-reactives and organic peroxides.<br />\n- <strong>Flame Over Circle</strong>: oxidizers, which can cause or intensify fire. Calcium hypochlorite, concentrated hydrogen peroxide.<br />\n- <strong>Environment</strong> (dead tree and fish): toxic to aquatic life. OSHA does not require this one, but it matters near streams and storm drains.<br />\n- <strong>Skull and Crossbones</strong>: acute toxicity. Fatal or toxic in small amounts by swallowing, skin contact or inhalation. Chlorine gas, some pesticides.</p>\n<p>A product can carry more than one pictogram.</p>"}, {"heading": "Secondary container labeling", "html": "<p>Most unlabeled containers OSHA finds are not supplier drums. They are the spray bottles, buckets and small tanks that crews fill themselves. These <strong>secondary containers</strong> need labels too.</p>\n<p>A workplace label must carry at least the <strong>product identifier</strong> plus words, pictures or symbols that give general information about the hazards. The simplest approach is to copy the supplier label.</p>\n<p>There is one narrow exception. A <strong>portable container</strong> you fill from a labeled container does not need a label if the contents are for your <strong>immediate use</strong> and stay in your control. Once it is set down for someone else or stored, it needs a label.</p>\n<p>Good habits:<br />\n- Never put a chemical in a food or drink container.<br />\n- Label the container before you fill it.<br />\n- Replace labels that are faded, torn or soaked through.<br />\n- If no one knows what is in a container, do not sniff it or guess. Tag it and report it.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants handle some of the most hazardous chemicals in the city. <strong>Chlorine gas</strong> in cylinders is acutely toxic and under pressure. <strong>Sodium hypochlorite</strong> (bleach, usually 10 to 15 percent) is corrosive and releases chlorine gas if it contacts acid. Know where the SDSs and eyewashes are, and never mix chemicals. Solid waste and stormwater crews find unknown containers dumped at curbs and outfalls. Treat them as hazardous until identified.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Fleet shops stock fuels, brake cleaner, parts washer solvent, degreasers, antifreeze, paint and welding gases. Update the SDS file when a vendor changes products. Streets crews use asphalt products, cold patch and crack sealer, which carry burn and vapor hazards. Forestry crews apply herbicides, where the EPA label and the SDS both matter. Snow and ice crews handle rock salt, calcium chloride and brine, which irritate skin and eyes.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Substations hold lead-acid batteries with corrosive sulfuric acid, so an eyewash and acid resistant PPE must be close by. Transformer oil is combustible, and older units may contain PCBs. SF6 breakers contain gas under pressure. Line trucks carry solvents and sealants, and those small cans need labels and SDSs just like shop drums.</p>\n<p>For every division the questions are the same: Do you know what the product is? Where is its SDS right now? Is the container labeled? If any answer is no, fix it first.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["You have the right to know the hazards of every chemical you work with, and the department must back that up with a written program, a chemical list, labels, SDSs and training.", "Every SDS has the same 16 sections, and Sections 2, 4, 7 and 8 answer most field questions about hazards, first aid, handling and PPE.", "SDSs must be readily accessible to you in your work area during every shift, not locked in an office.", "A GHS label has six elements: product identifier, signal word, hazard statements, precautionary statements, pictograms and supplier information.", "Danger marks the more severe hazards and Warning the less severe, and the nine pictograms show the type of hazard at a glance.", "Secondary containers need a label with the product identifier and hazard information unless the container is for your immediate use and stays in your control.", "Training is required at initial assignment and whenever a new chemical hazard enters your work area.", "The most common HazCom citations, no written program, no SDS, no training and unlabeled containers, are all preventable."], "questions": [{"text": "How many sections are on a Safety Data Sheet in the format required by the HazCom standard?", "options": ["8", "12", "16", "20"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "HazCom requires a 16-section SDS in a fixed order, which makes it easy to find the same information on any sheet."}, {"text": "Which signal word on a GHS label indicates the more severe hazard?", "options": ["Caution", "Danger", "Warning", "Hazardous"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Danger is reserved for the more severe hazards and Warning for the less severe. A label carries only one signal word."}, {"text": "A mechanic pours brake cleaner from a labeled drum into a small unlabeled can. When does that can need a label?", "options": ["Never, portable containers are always exempt from labeling", "Only if the can holds more than one gallon", "Only if the brake cleaner is flammable", "If it is set down for another worker, passed to another shift or stored instead of used right away"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The portable container exception applies only when the contents are for the immediate use of the person who filled it. Once the container leaves that person's control, it needs a workplace label."}, {"text": "Which item must be included in the written hazard communication program?", "options": ["A list of the hazardous chemicals known to be present in the workplace", "A copy of every employee's medical records", "Purchase receipts for every chemical bought that year", "The phone number of the nearest OSHA area office"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The written program must include a chemical inventory that uses the same product identifiers found on labels and SDSs, along with how the department handles labels, SDSs, training, non-routine tasks and unlabeled pipes."}, {"text": "What does the skull and crossbones pictogram tell you about a product?", "options": ["It is corrosive to metal", "It is an oxidizer that can intensify a fire", "It is a gas stored under pressure", "It can be fatal or toxic in small amounts if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed through the skin"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The skull and crossbones marks acute toxicity, meaning the product can cause death or serious harm from a small exposure. Chlorine gas and some pesticides carry it."}, {"text": "When must an employee receive HazCom training?", "options": ["Only once, at the time of hire", "At initial assignment and whenever a new chemical hazard is introduced into the work area", "Only after an exposure incident or spill", "Once every five years"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard requires training at initial assignment and again whenever a new hazard the employee has not been trained on enters the work area. Annual refreshers are good practice, but those two events are the triggers in the rule."}, {"text": "Which of these meets the requirement for Safety Data Sheet access?", "options": ["SDSs are kept in a locked office and provided on written request", "SDSs are stored at city hall and sent to the plant when needed", "SDSs are available to employees in their work area during every shift, with a backup if the electronic system fails", "SDSs are kept only by the chemical supplier, who will send one on request"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "SDSs must be readily accessible during each work shift to employees when they are in their work areas. Electronic access is fine as long as there are no barriers to immediate access and there is a backup."}, {"text": "What hazard does the flame over circle pictogram represent?", "options": ["Oxidizer", "Flammable liquid", "Explosive", "Gas under pressure"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The flame over circle marks oxidizers, which can cause or intensify a fire. The plain flame marks flammables, the exploding bomb marks explosives and the gas cylinder marks gases under pressure."}, {"text": "A worker at the water plant is splashed with a chemical. Which SDS section tells you what to do right away?", "options": ["Section 2, Hazard identification", "Section 4, First-aid measures", "Section 9, Physical and chemical properties", "Section 14, Transport information"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 4 of every SDS gives first-aid measures for skin, eye, inhalation and ingestion exposures. Section 2 describes the hazards but not the immediate response."}, {"text": "At a wastewater plant, what happens if sodium hypochlorite is accidentally mixed with an acid?", "options": ["The two neutralize into a harmless salt solution", "It forms a stronger disinfectant that is safe to use", "It releases toxic chlorine gas", "Nothing, because sodium hypochlorite is not reactive"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Sodium hypochlorite reacts with acids to release chlorine gas, which is acutely toxic. Section 10 of the SDS lists incompatible materials like this, which is why chemicals are never mixed or stored carelessly."}]}, {"slug": "lockout-tagout", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy (Lockout/Tagout)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "standard_title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 3, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Lockout/tagout protects workers from the unexpected startup of machines or the release of stored energy while they service or maintain equipment. This course explains the energy control program required by 29 CFR 1910.147, the roles of authorized and affected employees, and the step-by-step sequence for locking out, verifying zero energy and safely restoring equipment to service. Examples come from treatment plants, solid waste operations, fleet shops, traffic signal work and electric service work.", "objectives": ["Explain the purpose of lockout/tagout and name the types of hazardous energy found in public works equipment.", "Describe the roles of authorized, affected and other employees under an energy control program.", "Perform the lockout sequence in order, including releasing stored energy and verifying zero energy before work begins.", "Explain when tags alone may be used and why a tag gives less protection than a lock.", "Apply the rules for group lockout, shift changes and removing a lock when its owner is unavailable.", "Identify the training, retraining and annual inspection requirements that keep an energy control program current."], "sections": [{"heading": "What lockout/tagout is for", "html": "<p><strong>Lockout/tagout (LOTO)</strong> keeps a machine from starting, energizing or releasing stored energy while someone is servicing or maintaining it. 29 CFR 1910.147 covers servicing and maintenance in general industry where unexpected startup or the release of stored energy could injure an employee.</p>\n<p>The hazard is easy to picture. A pump restarts while a mechanic has a hand inside the volute. A baler ram cycles while someone clears a jam. A dump body drifts down on a technician underneath it. In each case the energy was still there, or came back, while a person was in the danger zone.</p>\n<p>The standard does not apply to cord-and-plug equipment when the plug is pulled and stays under the exclusive control of the person doing the work. Everything else that involves reaching into, under or behind a machine for servicing is covered.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Lockout/tagout appears on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards year after year. Inspectors usually find the same problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No machine-specific procedures.</strong> A general policy exists, but no written steps for each machine say which energy sources to isolate and how to verify zero energy.</li>\n<li><strong>No periodic inspection.</strong> Procedures were never inspected at least annually, or no certification of the inspection exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Training gaps.</strong> Authorized employees were never trained or retrained, or affected employees were never told what the locks and tags mean.</li>\n<li><strong>Procedures not used.</strong> Locks and tags exist, but crews clear jams with only the stop button pressed.</li>\n<li><strong>No program at all.</strong> Smaller shops and plants sometimes have nothing on paper.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works departments face every one of these risks because crews maintain so many different machines across plants, shops and field equipment.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of hazardous energy", "html": "<p>Energy that can hurt you is not limited to electricity. Before any servicing task, identify every source and how much of it there is.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> motors, control circuits, generators, and capacitors that hold a charge after power is off.</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanical:</strong> rotating shafts, flywheels, blades and belts that keep moving after shutdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydraulic:</strong> pressurized fluid in refuse truck packers, dump bodies, plow lifts and loader arms.</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumatic:</strong> compressed air in shop lines, air brakes and actuators.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical:</strong> chlorine, sulfur dioxide, caustics and fuels in piping and tanks.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal:</strong> steam, hot water, engine parts and exhaust.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity:</strong> raised dump bodies, plow blades, buckets, gates and anything that can fall or drift down.</li>\n<li><strong>Stored energy:</strong> compressed springs, charged capacitors, pressure trapped in lines and accumulators, and elevated parts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A machine often has more than one source. A refuse truck has the engine, hydraulics, air brakes and a tailgate that gravity will close.</p>"}, {"heading": "The energy control program and who does what", "html": "<p>The standard requires a written <strong>energy control program</strong> with three parts: energy control procedures, employee training and periodic inspections.</p>\n<p><strong>Written procedures</strong> must be specific to each machine or group of similar machines. Each one states the steps to shut down, isolate, block and secure the equipment, the steps for placing, removing and transferring locks and tags, and how to test that isolation worked. A general policy on the wall does not meet this requirement.</p>\n<p>Three groups of people are defined:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authorized employees</strong> apply locks and tags and perform the servicing. Only they may lock out equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Affected employees</strong> operate the equipment or work in the area where it is serviced. They must know what the locks and tags mean and never try to restart locked-out equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Other employees</strong> work where LOTO may be used. They are told never to restart locked-out equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>energy isolating device</strong> is a physical device such as a disconnect switch, circuit breaker, line valve or block. Push buttons, selector switches, emergency stops and other control circuit devices are <strong>not</strong> energy isolating devices and cannot be used for lockout.</p>"}, {"heading": "The lockout sequence, step by step", "html": "<p>Follow the written procedure in order. Skipping a step is how injuries happen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1. Notify</strong> affected employees that the equipment will be shut down and locked out.</li>\n<li><strong>2. Prepare for shutdown.</strong> Know the types and magnitude of energy and how each source is controlled.</li>\n<li><strong>3. Shut down</strong> the equipment using its normal stopping procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>4. Isolate</strong> every energy source: open the disconnect, close and chain the valve, pull the fuses as the procedure directs.</li>\n<li><strong>5. Apply locks and tags</strong> to each isolating device. Each authorized employee applies a personal lock.</li>\n<li><strong>6. Release or restrain stored energy.</strong> Bleed pressure, let moving parts stop, discharge capacitors, lower or block raised parts. If energy can build back up, keep checking until the job is done.</li>\n<li><strong>7. Verify zero energy.</strong> Try to start the machine with the normal controls, test with a meter where appropriate and confirm that nothing moves. Return the controls to off afterward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only then does work begin.</p>\n<p>To <strong>restore service</strong>, remove tools, replace guards and confirm everyone is clear. Each person removes their own lock, affected employees are notified, and then the equipment is reenergized. If the machine must be energized briefly for testing, clear the area, remove the locks, test, then deenergize and reapply the locks before continuing work.</p>"}, {"heading": "Locks, tags, group lockout and shift changes", "html": "<p><strong>Lockout is the rule.</strong> When an energy isolating device can accept a lock, it must be locked. A <strong>tagout</strong> alone is allowed only when the device cannot be locked, or when the employer can show that tags plus extra measures give protection equal to a lock, such as removing a fuse, blocking a controlling switch or removing a valve handle.</p>\n<p>A tag is a warning, not a restraint. It must be legible, durable, attached with a self-locking, nonreusable fastener, and it must name the person who placed it. Treat a tag as seriously as a lock and never remove or bypass one.</p>\n<p><strong>Group lockout.</strong> When a crew works together, one authorized employee takes primary responsibility, usually with a group lockbox. The keys to the isolation locks go in the box, and each crew member adds a personal lock to the box when work starts and removes it when they finish.</p>\n<p><strong>Shift changes.</strong> The program must have a procedure for handing off locks between off-going and on-coming employees so protection never lapses.</p>\n<p><strong>Lock removal when the owner is unavailable.</strong> Only the person who applied a lock may remove it. If that employee is not at the facility, the lock may be removed only under a documented procedure: verify the employee is not on site, make every reasonable effort to contact them, and make sure they know the lock is gone before they return to work.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspections, training and retraining", "html": "<p><strong>Periodic inspection.</strong> Each energy control procedure must be inspected at least annually by an authorized employee who is not using that procedure at the time. The inspector watches the procedure being used, corrects any deviations and reviews responsibilities with each authorized employee. The inspection is <strong>certified</strong> in writing with the equipment, the date, the employees included and the inspector's name.</p>\n<p><strong>Training.</strong> Authorized employees learn to recognize hazardous energy, its type and magnitude, and how to isolate and control it. Affected employees learn the purpose and use of the procedure. All other employees learn that locked or tagged equipment is never to be restarted. The employer certifies training with each employee's name and the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Retraining</strong> is required when job assignments change, when equipment or processes change in a way that creates a new hazard, when procedures change, or when an inspection shows an employee is not following the procedure.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Wastewater and water plants have pumps, clarifier drives, blowers, screens and chemical feed systems. Lock out the motor disconnect, close and tag the suction and discharge valves, bleed pressure and block any rake arm or gate that gravity can move. At solid waste transfer stations, the conveyor and baler carry electrical, hydraulic and gravity energy. Clear jams only after full lockout, never with the machine merely stopped by a button.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Refuse trucks, snowplows and dump trucks hold hydraulic pressure and raised loads. Lower the body or plow, or install the manufacturer's body prop or pin, bleed the hydraulics and remove the key before working underneath. Chipper discs and drums coast after shutdown. Wait for them to stop and disable the engine before clearing the throat or changing knives. Traffic signal cabinets, facility generators and automatic transfer switches need the feeder breaker and the generator source locked out, because a transfer switch can start the generator and energize a bus on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Work on electric power generation, transmission and distribution installations follows 29 CFR 1910.269(d) and (m), which rely on clearances, system operator tags and protective grounds, rather than 1910.147. The sequence is the same in spirit: notify, deenergize, isolate, lock or tag, then test and ground before touching. Shop equipment, hydraulic tools and building systems still fall under 1910.147.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Lockout/tagout exists to prevent unexpected startup or the release of stored energy while equipment is being serviced or maintained.", "Every machine needs a written, machine-specific procedure that names the energy sources, the isolating devices and how to verify zero energy.", "Only authorized employees apply locks and tags, and only the person who applied a lock may remove it, except under a documented absent-employee procedure.", "Never start work until stored energy is released or restrained and you have tried to start the machine to prove it is dead.", "Push buttons and emergency stops are not energy isolating devices; a lock belongs on the disconnect, breaker or valve.", "Use a lock whenever the device can accept one; a tag alone is allowed only when the device cannot be locked or extra measures give equal protection.", "Each procedure must be inspected at least annually and the inspection certified, and training must be refreshed whenever jobs, equipment or procedures change."], "questions": [{"text": "What is the main purpose of lockout/tagout under 29 CFR 1910.147?", "options": ["To make sure machines are repaired only by licensed contractors", "To document equipment downtime for maintenance records", "To prevent unexpected startup or the release of stored energy while equipment is serviced", "To keep operators from running equipment above its rated speed"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard exists to keep machines from energizing, starting up or releasing stored energy while someone is performing servicing or maintenance."}, {"text": "Which of these counts as an energy isolating device?", "options": ["A manually operated disconnect switch", "The emergency stop button on the control panel", "A selector switch turned to off", "A push button labeled stop"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Energy isolating devices physically block the flow of energy, such as disconnects, breakers, line valves and blocks. Push buttons, selector switches and emergency stops are control circuit devices and cannot be used for lockout."}, {"text": "After locks are applied and stored energy is released, what must happen before work begins?", "options": ["The supervisor signs the work order", "Affected employees are sent home", "The equipment is cleaned and lubricated", "The authorized employee tries to start the equipment to verify it is deenergized"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Verification is the last step before work starts. Trying the normal start controls, and testing with a meter where appropriate, proves the isolation actually worked."}, {"text": "When is a tag alone allowed instead of a lock?", "options": ["Whenever the job will take less than one hour", "When the energy isolating device cannot accept a lock, or when tagout plus extra measures gives protection equal to a lock", "When the authorized employee prefers tags", "When the machine has been serviced before without incident"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Lockout is required whenever the device can be locked. Tagout alone is limited to devices that cannot accept a lock, or to cases where the employer shows that extra measures make tagout as protective as a lock."}, {"text": "How often must each energy control procedure be inspected, and who performs the inspection?", "options": ["At least annually, by an authorized employee who is not using that procedure at the time", "Every five years, by an outside consultant", "Monthly, by the affected employees who operate the machine", "Only after an injury, by the plant manager"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires a periodic inspection at least annually by an authorized employee other than those using the procedure, and the inspection must be certified in writing."}, {"text": "A mechanic locked out a pump and left for the day with the lock still on. What is required before anyone else removes that lock?", "options": ["Nothing, as long as the shift supervisor has a master key", "A second mechanic applies a new lock and cuts the old one", "The affected operator confirms the pump is needed back in service", "Verify the mechanic is not on site, make every reasonable effort to contact them, and ensure they know the lock is gone before they return to work, following a documented procedure"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Only the person who applied a lock may remove it. The exception requires a documented procedure that includes verifying the employee is not at the facility, trying to contact them and informing them before they resume work."}, {"text": "A dump body is raised and the truck is shut off with the key removed. What hazardous energy is still present?", "options": ["Thermal energy from the hydraulic fluid", "Gravity, because the body can drift or fall if the hydraulics bleed down", "No hazardous energy remains once the engine is off", "Chemical energy in the fuel tank"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A raised body or plow is stored gravitational energy. It must be lowered or secured with the body prop or pin before anyone works underneath."}, {"text": "An operator runs the clarifier drive but never performs maintenance on it. Under the standard, this person is which type of employee?", "options": ["Authorized employee", "Qualified employee", "Affected employee", "Competent person"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An affected employee operates or uses the equipment being serviced, or works in the area where it is serviced. They must understand the procedure but do not apply locks."}, {"text": "In a group lockout, how does each crew member stay protected?", "options": ["Each member places a personal lock on the group lockbox when starting work and removes it when finished", "The lead worker's single lock covers everyone on the crew", "Each member keeps a copy of the lockbox key in their pocket", "The crew signs a sign-in sheet instead of using locks"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Group lockout gives one authorized employee primary responsibility, but every member still attaches a personal lock to the group device or lockbox so no one can reenergize the equipment while they are exposed."}, {"text": "A line crew is deenergizing a distribution circuit to replace a pole. Which standard governs that hazardous energy control work?", "options": ["29 CFR 1910.147 only", "29 CFR 1910.212", "29 CFR 1910.178", "29 CFR 1910.269(d) and (m)"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Electric power generation, transmission and distribution work follows 1910.269(d) and (m) rather than 1910.147, though the sequence of notify, deenergize, isolate, lock or tag and test is the same in spirit."}]}, {"slug": "scaffolding", "title": "Scaffolding", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "standard_title": "Scaffolding, general requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 4, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Scaffolds put public works crews at height over pump stations, clarifiers, bridge decks, substation structures and facility walls. This course covers the parts of 29 CFR 1926.451 that matter most in the field: capacity, planking, foundations, access, guardrails, power line clearance and the competent person inspection. It also reviews the mistakes that draw the most OSHA citations so you can spot them before anyone climbs.", "objectives": ["Explain the capacity rules for scaffolds, suspension ropes and guardrails.", "Describe how a scaffold platform must be planked, supported and restrained from tipping.", "Identify acceptable means of access and the fall protection required above 10 feet.", "Apply the minimum clearance distances between scaffolds and energized power lines.", "Recognize when a competent person must inspect a scaffold and when work must stop for weather.", "Spot the most commonly cited scaffold violations on a public works job."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Scaffold violations appear on OSHA's list of most frequently cited standards year after year. Scaffolds are often set up quickly, by crews who do it a few times a year, for a job that lasts a day or two. That is exactly when guardrails get skipped and nobody is named as the <strong>competent person</strong>.</p>\n<p>The citations inspectors write most often are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Missing or incomplete <strong>guardrails</strong> on platforms more than 10 feet up.</li>\n<li>Improper <strong>planking</strong>: gaps, short overlaps, or levels that are not fully decked.</li>\n<li>Unsafe <strong>access</strong>: climbing cross braces or frames instead of using a ladder or stair.</li>\n<li>No <strong>competent person inspection</strong> before the shift.</li>\n<li>Poor <strong>foundations</strong>, such as legs resting on blocks or soft ground.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works crews face the same hazards when they repair a bridge wall or repaint a treatment building. Although 1926.451 is a construction standard, OSHA's general industry rule at 1910.27(a) requires scaffolds used in general industry to meet the same requirements, so these rules apply to maintenance jobs too.</p>"}, {"heading": "Capacity and platform construction", "html": "<p>Every scaffold and every scaffold component must support its own weight plus at least <strong>4 times the maximum intended load</strong> without failing (1926.451(a)(1)). The maximum intended load is everyone on the platform plus their tools and materials. On suspension scaffolds, each rope and its hardware must support at least <strong>6 times</strong> the maximum intended load (1926.451(a)(4)).</p>\n<p>Platforms must be built so nobody can step through or off the edge (1926.451(b)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each working level must be <strong>fully planked or decked</strong> between the front uprights and the guardrail supports.</li>\n<li>Gaps between planks, and between the platform and the uprights, may be no more than <strong>1 inch</strong> wide.</li>\n<li>Platforms and walkways must be at least <strong>18 inches wide</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each plank end must extend at least <strong>6 inches</strong> past the centerline of its support unless cleated or hooked, and no more than <strong>12 inches</strong> on platforms 10 feet or shorter, or <strong>18 inches</strong> on longer platforms, unless the overhang is guarded.</li>\n<li>Where planks overlap to make a long platform, the overlap must be at least <strong>12 inches</strong> and must sit over a support.</li>\n<li>Use scaffold-grade planks or manufactured decks.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Foundations, stability and tipping", "html": "<p>A scaffold is only as good as what it stands on. Supported scaffold legs and frames must rest on <strong>base plates</strong> and <strong>mud sills</strong> or another firm foundation (1926.451(c)(2)). Footings must be level, sound, rigid and able to carry the loaded scaffold without settling. Never set a leg on bricks, blocks, boxes, barrels or other unstable objects. Legs must be plumb and braced so the scaffold cannot sway.</p>\n<p>Tall, narrow scaffolds tip. When the <strong>height to base width ratio is more than 4 to 1</strong> (counting outriggers, if used), the scaffold must be restrained with <strong>guys, ties or braces</strong> to the structure (1926.451(c)(1)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Install the first tie at the closest horizontal member to the 4 to 1 height. A scaffold 5 feet wide needs its first tie at about 20 feet.</li>\n<li>Repeat ties every 20 feet or less going up for scaffolds 3 feet wide or narrower, and every 26 feet or less for wider scaffolds, with ties at each end and no more than 30 feet apart.</li>\n<li>A cantilevered or other off-center load also requires ties, braces or outriggers.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Safe access", "html": "<p>If the platform is more than <strong>2 feet</strong> above or below the point of access, crews need a proper way up (1926.451(e)(1)). Acceptable means include portable, hook-on or attachable ladders, stair towers, ramps and walkways, built-in frame ladders designed for climbing, or direct access from another scaffold or structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Cross braces are never a means of access.</strong> Climbing the X braces is one of the most common violations inspectors see, and many scaffold falls happen getting on and off.</p>\n<p>Access rules worth remembering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hook-on and attachable ladders need their bottom rung no more than 24 inches above the supporting level.</li>\n<li>Frame rungs may be climbed only if the manufacturer designed that frame as a ladder.</li>\n<li>Ladders, boxes, barrels and other makeshift devices may not be used on top of a platform to gain height (1926.451(f)(14) and (f)(15)).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Fall protection, guardrails and falling objects", "html": "<p>Each employee on a scaffold platform more than <strong>10 feet above a lower level</strong> must be protected from falling (1926.451(g)(1)). For most supported scaffolds that means a <strong>guardrail system</strong>. Where guardrails are not practical, a personal fall arrest system tied to a proper anchorage is used.</p>\n<p>Guardrail requirements (1926.451(g)(4)):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toprail</strong> between <strong>38 and 45 inches</strong> above the platform on supported scaffolds placed in service after January 1, 2000, able to withstand 200 pounds of force.</li>\n<li><strong>Midrail</strong> about halfway between the toprail and the platform, able to withstand 150 pounds.</li>\n<li>Guardrails on all open sides and ends.</li>\n<li>Cross bracing may serve as a midrail if the braces cross between 20 and 30 inches above the platform, or as a toprail if they cross between 38 and 48 inches, but not both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hard hats are required on and around scaffolds. Where objects could fall on people below, add <strong>toeboards</strong> at least <strong>3.5 inches</strong> high with no more than a quarter inch gap at the platform (1926.451(h)), or use screens, nets, canopies or a barricaded zone below.</p>"}, {"heading": "Power line clearance", "html": "<p>Scaffolds are usually metal, and the people on them handle conductive pipe and tools. Before erecting, moving or working from a scaffold near overhead lines, apply the clearance table in 1926.451(f)(6):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uninsulated lines</strong> up to 50 kV: at least <strong>10 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Insulated lines</strong> under 300 volts: at least <strong>3 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Insulated lines</strong> 300 volts to 50 kV: at least <strong>10 feet</strong>.</li>\n<li>Any line over 50 kV: 10 feet plus 0.4 inch for each kV over 50, or two times the length of the line insulator, and never less than 10 feet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The distance applies to the scaffold and to any conductive material handled on it. Treat every line as uninsulated and energized unless the utility has confirmed otherwise. The weatherproof covering on many distribution conductors is not insulation.</p>\n<p>Crews may work closer only after the utility or system operator has <strong>de-energized the lines, relocated them or installed protective coverings</strong>. For Electric Services, that means a documented hold from the system operator. For Environmental and Public Services crews, call the utility and get the answer before the first frame goes up.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection, training, mobile scaffolds and weather", "html": "<p>A <strong>competent person</strong> must inspect the scaffold for visible defects <strong>before each work shift</strong> and after any event that could affect its structural integrity, such as a vehicle strike, a storm or an overload (1926.451(f)(3)). A competent person can identify scaffold hazards and has authority to correct them. Damaged parts must be repaired, replaced or removed from service right away. Scaffolds may be erected, moved, dismantled or altered only under a competent person's direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Training</strong> (1926.454): everyone who works from a scaffold must be trained by a qualified person on the electrical, fall and falling object hazards, the fall protection in use, material handling and the maximum intended load. Erectors, dismantlers and inspectors need training from a competent person.</p>\n<p><strong>Mobile scaffolds</strong> (1926.452(w)): lock the casters before anyone climbs. Push from near the base, never more than 5 feet up, and warn everyone on the platform first. Riding is allowed only when the surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of holes, the height to base ratio during the move is 2 to 1 or less, and nobody is on a part that extends past the wheels.</p>\n<p><strong>Weather</strong>: no scaffold work during storms or high winds unless the competent person decides it is safe and workers are protected by personal fall arrest or wind screens (1926.451(f)(12)). Clear snow and ice before use.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p>Public works scaffold jobs tend to be short and varied. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Environmental Services</strong>: replacing clarifier weirs, repairing a pump station roof, painting digester walls, working over an open channel. Surfaces are wet and a fall may end in water or a tank, so the rescue plan belongs in the job briefing.</li>\n<li><strong>Public Services</strong>: bridge rail and culvert headwall repair, replacing lights in a fleet garage or gym, exterior painting, sign and signal work from a rolling tower. Mobile scaffolds on sloped aprons tip easily. Level them with screw jacks and lock the casters.</li>\n<li><strong>Electric Services</strong>: substation structure painting and bus work with equipment de-energized and grounded, and support for contractors building new structures. Clearance rules and the system operator's hold control the job.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before anyone climbs, ask five questions. Who is the competent person, and did they inspect it this shift? Is every level fully planked with proper overlap? Is there a ladder or stair, not a cross brace? Are guardrails up above 10 feet? How far is the nearest energized line? If any answer is wrong, fix it on the ground. Rented scaffolds are no exception; your competent person still owns the inspection.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Scaffolds must support at least 4 times the maximum intended load, and suspension ropes at least 6 times.", "Platforms must be fully planked, at least 18 inches wide, with gaps of no more than 1 inch and plank ends extending 6 to 12 inches past the support.", "Legs rest on base plates and mud sills on level, sound footing, never on blocks, boxes or barrels.", "Tie, guy or brace any scaffold taller than 4 times its base width.", "Get on and off with a ladder, stair tower or ramp; cross braces are never access.", "Above 10 feet, use guardrails with a toprail 38 to 45 inches high and a midrail, or personal fall arrest, plus toeboards where objects could fall on people.", "Stay at least 10 feet from uninsulated lines up to 50 kV, and farther for higher voltages, unless the utility has de-energized, moved or covered them.", "A competent person inspects every scaffold before each shift and after anything that could damage it."], "questions": [{"text": "A scaffold and each of its components must be able to support its own weight plus at least how many times the maximum intended load?", "options": ["2 times", "3 times", "4 times", "6 times"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.451(a)(1) requires every scaffold and scaffold component to support, without failure, its own weight plus at least 4 times the maximum intended load. The 6 times figure applies to suspension ropes and their hardware."}, {"text": "Which platform meets the planking requirements of 1926.451(b)?", "options": ["A fully decked platform 20 inches wide with no gap wider than 1 inch between planks", "A platform with a 3 inch gap left open along the uprights so tools can be passed up", "A 16 inch wide platform made of two planks laid side by side", "A platform where the planks overlap by 8 inches midway between the supports"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Platforms must be fully planked and at least 18 inches wide, with gaps of no more than 1 inch. Overlaps must be at least 12 inches and must occur over a support."}, {"text": "Which of these is an acceptable foundation for a supported scaffold leg?", "options": ["A stack of two concrete blocks on packed gravel", "An empty 55 gallon drum turned upside down", "A wood pallet laid flat on asphalt", "A base plate on a mud sill set on level, compacted ground"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "1926.451(c)(2) requires legs to bear on base plates and mud sills or another firm, level, rigid foundation. Blocks, drums, pallets and other unstable objects may not be used."}, {"text": "A supported scaffold is 5 feet wide at the base with no outriggers. At about what height must it first be tied, guyed or braced to prevent tipping?", "options": ["10 feet", "20 feet", "26 feet", "30 feet"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A height to base width ratio of more than 4 to 1 requires restraint. Four times 5 feet is 20 feet, so the first tie goes at the closest horizontal member to that height."}, {"text": "A crew needs to reach a platform 6 feet above the ground. Which means of access is allowed under 1926.451(e)?", "options": ["A hook-on ladder with its bottom rung no more than 24 inches above the supporting level", "The cross braces, as long as the worker faces the scaffold", "The end frame, since any horizontal member can serve as a rung", "A 5 gallon bucket placed next to the lowest frame member"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Portable, hook-on and attachable ladders, stair towers, ramps and built-in access designed for climbing are acceptable. Cross braces are never a means of access, and frame members may be climbed only when designed as ladder rungs."}, {"text": "On a supported scaffold placed in service after January 1, 2000, the top edge of the toprail must be installed how high above the platform?", "options": ["30 to 36 inches", "36 to 42 inches", "38 to 45 inches", "42 to 48 inches"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.451(g)(4)(ii) sets the toprail height at 38 to 45 inches for supported scaffolds placed in service after January 1, 2000, with a midrail about halfway between the toprail and the platform."}, {"text": "A toeboard used to protect people below a scaffold from falling objects must be at least how tall?", "options": ["2 inches", "3.5 inches", "4 inches", "6 inches"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "1926.451(h)(4)(ii) requires toeboards at least 3.5 inches high, securely fastened at the outer edge of the platform with no more than a quarter inch gap above the walking surface."}, {"text": "A crew is setting up a frame scaffold under an uninsulated 12.47 kV distribution line. What is the minimum clearance that must be kept between the scaffold, or any conductive material handled on it, and the line?", "options": ["3 feet", "6 feet", "8 feet", "10 feet"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "For uninsulated lines up to 50 kV, 1926.451(f)(6) requires at least 10 feet of clearance. The 3 foot distance applies only to insulated lines under 300 volts."}, {"text": "When must a competent person inspect a scaffold for visible defects?", "options": ["Before each work shift and after any occurrence that could affect its structural integrity", "Once when it is first erected and again when it is dismantled", "Weekly, or whenever a worker reports a problem", "Only after a storm, vehicle strike or other damaging event"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1926.451(f)(3) requires inspection before each work shift and after any event that could affect the scaffold's integrity. Erection-only or weekly checks do not meet the rule."}, {"text": "Workers may ride a mobile scaffold while it is being moved only if certain conditions are met. Which is one of them?", "options": ["The crew leader gives a verbal warning after the move begins", "The scaffold is pushed from a point at least 6 feet above the ground for better leverage", "The surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of pits, holes and obstructions", "The height to base width ratio during the move is no more than 4 to 1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1926.452(w)(6) allows riding only when the surface is within 3 degrees of level and free of holes and obstructions, the height to base ratio during the move is 2 to 1 or less, and no one is on a part that extends past the wheels."}]}, {"slug": "ladders", "title": "Ladders", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "standard_title": "Ladders (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 5, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Ladders are on nearly every public works truck and in every shop, which is why ladder violations stay on OSHA's Top 10 list every year. This course covers how to pick the right ladder, inspect it, set it up at the correct angle, climb it safely and know when a ladder is the wrong tool. It also explains the rules for fixed ladders on tanks and structures and the training required under 1926.1060.", "objectives": ["Select a ladder with the correct duty rating and material for the task, including nonconductive ladders near electrical hazards.", "Inspect a ladder before use, identify defects that require removal from service, and tag the ladder so no one else uses it.", "Set up an extension ladder at the 4 to 1 angle with the side rails extending at least 3 feet above the landing or secured with a grab device.", "Apply the use rules in 1926.1053(b), including three points of contact, facing the ladder and staying off the top steps.", "Recognize when fixed ladders on tanks and structures require cages, wells or personal fall arrest systems.", "Describe the ladder training required under 1926.1060 and how it is documented."], "sections": [{"heading": "Choosing the right ladder", "html": "<p>Ladder safety starts before you climb. Three choices decide whether the ladder can do the job.</p>\n<p><strong>Duty rating.</strong> Every portable ladder carries a label with its duty rating, the maximum load it can carry, including you, your tools and your materials. Ratings run from Type III (200 pounds) up to Type IAA (375 pounds). The department buys Type IA (300 pounds) and IAA ladders for field work. Add up your body weight, tool belt and whatever you are carrying before you choose.</p>\n<p><strong>Material.</strong> Near any electrical hazard, use a <strong>nonconductive fiberglass ladder</strong>. Section 1926.1053(b)(12) requires nonconductive side rails when the employee or the ladder could contact exposed energized equipment. <strong>Never use a metal ladder near energized lines or equipment</strong>, including service drops, street light poles, signal cabinets and anything in a substation. Aluminum conducts, and so does wet wood.</p>\n<p><strong>Type.</strong> A stepladder is for free-standing work, fully opened with the spreaders locked, never leaned against a wall. An extension ladder is for reaching a landing or wall. Either must be long enough to work from without standing on the top steps.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Ladder citations are common because ladders are everywhere and the mistakes are visible from the street.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often under 1926.1053 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(b)(1)</strong> Side rails not extending at least 3 feet above the upper landing, with no grab device or securing in its place.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(4)</strong> Ladders used for something other than their designed purpose, such as a closed stepladder leaned against a wall.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(13)</strong> Workers standing on the top step or cap of a stepladder.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(16)</strong> Defective ladders left in service instead of being tagged and removed.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(22)</strong> Workers carrying loads that could cause them to lose their balance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of these take special equipment to fix. They take the extra minute to pick the right ladder, set it correctly and tie it off. Ladder falls are usually short falls, and short falls still break bones and end careers.</p>"}, {"heading": "Inspection and removing defective ladders", "html": "<p>Section 1926.1053(b)(15) requires a competent person to inspect ladders periodically and after any event that could affect their safe use, such as being dropped or run over. Every user should also look the ladder over before each climb. Check for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cracked, split, bent or dented side rails.</li>\n<li>Missing, loose, bent or broken rungs, cleats or steps.</li>\n<li>Worn or missing nonslip feet.</li>\n<li>Loose rivets, bolts or hinges, spreaders that do not lock, and rung locks that do not hold.</li>\n<li>Oil, grease, mud or ice on rungs or rails.</li>\n<li>Fiberglass that is chalky, cracked or shows exposed fibers, which weakens the rail and reduces its insulating value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under 1926.1053(b)(16), a ladder with a structural defect must be <strong>immediately marked as defective or tagged 'Do Not Use'</strong> and <strong>withdrawn from service</strong> until it is repaired to its original design or discarded. Do not set it back on the truck rack for the next shift to grab. Tag it, pull it and tell your supervisor. Do not make field repairs with tape, wire or scrap lumber.</p>"}, {"heading": "Setting up the ladder", "html": "<p>Most ladder falls happen because the ladder moved. The setup rules exist to keep it still.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The 4 to 1 angle.</strong> Set a non-self-supporting ladder so the horizontal distance from the foot to the top support is <strong>one quarter of the working length</strong>. For a ladder reaching 16 feet up, the feet go 4 feet out.</li>\n<li><strong>Extend 3 feet above the landing.</strong> Side rails must <strong>extend at least 3 feet above an upper landing</strong>. If the ladder is too short, it must be <strong>secured at the top to a rigid support</strong> with a <strong>grab rail or grab device</strong> installed so you have something to hold while stepping on and off.</li>\n<li><strong>Firm, level footing.</strong> Section 1926.1053(b)(6) requires stable and level surfaces unless the ladder is secured against displacement. Use leg levelers on slopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Never on boxes, barrels or other unstable bases.</strong> The general industry rule, 1910.23(c), says this directly. Get a longer ladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure the ladder.</strong> Tie it off at the top, have someone foot the bottom, or both. In a passageway, doorway or driveway, secure or barricade it.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the top and bottom clear</strong> so you can step on and off without tripping.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Climbing and working from a ladder", "html": "<p>Once the ladder is set, the rules in 1926.1053(b) govern how you use it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Face the ladder</strong> going up and coming down.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep three points of contact</strong>: two hands and one foot, or two feet and one hand, on the ladder when climbing. Use at least one hand to grasp the ladder at all times.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not carry loads that could cause you to lose your balance.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Keep your belt buckle between the rails.</strong> Leaning out tips the ladder. Climb down and move it instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay off the top of a stepladder.</strong> The <strong>top or top step</strong> of a stepladder shall not be used as a step.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay off the top rungs of an extension ladder.</strong> Never stand above the highest standing level marked on the manufacturer's label. OSHA's ladder safety guidance is not to stand on the top three rungs of a straight or extension ladder. Need more height? Get a longer ladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not exceed the maximum intended load</strong>, which includes everything on the ladder with you.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not move, shift or extend a ladder while anyone is on it.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Use ladders only for their designed purpose</strong>, never as a scaffold plank or ramp, and <strong>do not tie ladders together</strong> unless they are designed for it.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Fixed ladders on tanks, towers and structures", "html": "<p>Public works facilities are full of fixed ladders: water towers, clarifiers, digesters, wet wells and shop mezzanines. Most are general industry walking-working surfaces, so <strong>29 CFR 1910.23</strong> (ladders) and <strong>1910.28</strong> (duty to have fall protection) apply alongside the construction rule.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Under 1926.1053(a)(19), a fixed ladder with a length of climb of <strong>24 feet or more</strong> must have a ladder safety device, a self-retracting lifeline, or a cage or well with the ladder broken into sections. Under 1910.28(b)(9), a fixed ladder extending <strong>more than 24 feet</strong> above a lower level must have a personal fall arrest system, a ladder safety system, a cage or a well. Cages and wells are being phased out: a ladder installed or replaced since November 19, 2018 needs a <strong>ladder safety system or PFAS</strong>, and every fixed ladder over 24 feet must have one by <strong>November 18, 2036</strong>.</li>\n<li>A ladder safety system uses a sleeve that locks onto a rail or cable if you fall. Be trained on the sleeve before you clip in.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A cage will not stop a fall. It only keeps you close to the ladder if you slip. Do not treat it as fall protection.</p>"}, {"heading": "Training under 1926.1060", "html": "<p>Section <strong>29 CFR 1926.1060</strong> requires the employer to provide a training program for each employee using ladders and stairways. The program must enable each worker to recognize ladder and stairway hazards and know the procedures that minimize them. Training must be given by a <strong>competent person</strong> and must cover, as applicable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nature of fall hazards in the work area.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for erecting, maintaining and disassembling the fall protection systems used.</li>\n<li>Proper construction, use, placement and care in handling of all ladders and stairways.</li>\n<li>The <strong>maximum intended load-carrying capacity</strong> of the ladders used.</li>\n<li>The standards in Subpart X themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Retraining is required as necessary so that workers keep the understanding the standard calls for. Unlike 1926.503, this section does not spell out a written certification record. The department documents it anyway, through this platform. Your completion record here, plus the ladder portion of your division's hands-on session, is your training record.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Fiberglass only, no exceptions. Line crews, substation technicians and meter technicians work around energized conductors, service drops, meter bases and padmount transformers every day. Keep the ladder and anything you carry outside the minimum approach distance, and never lean a ladder against a conductor, guy wire or pole-mounted enclosure.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Plant operators climb fixed ladders on clarifiers, digesters and elevated tanks, many of them over 24 feet. Know which ladders have a safety system and bring the right sleeve. Collection crews using portable ladders in manholes and vaults are also under confined space rules; the ladder is the escape route, so it must be secured and clear.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Signal and streetlight technicians near energized equipment follow the same fiberglass rule as Electric. Facilities staff working through a roof hatch must have the ladder extend 3 feet above the hatch or be secured with a grab bar. Forestry crews never set a ladder near overhead lines. Fleet mechanics working on truck bodies and plows should use a platform ladder or rolling stairs when a task takes more than a minute.</p>\n<p>For everyone: if the ladder in the truck is the wrong one, go get the right one. The trip back to the shop is cheaper than the fall.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Choose a ladder with a duty rating that covers you plus your tools and materials, and use nonconductive fiberglass anywhere there is an electrical hazard.", "Never use a metal ladder near energized lines or equipment.", "Inspect every ladder before use, and tag and remove any ladder with a structural defect so no one else climbs it.", "Set extension ladders at a 4 to 1 angle on firm, level footing, extend the rails at least 3 feet above the landing or secure the ladder with a grab device, and never set a ladder on boxes or barrels.", "Face the ladder, keep three points of contact, keep your belt buckle between the rails, and do not carry loads that could throw off your balance.", "Do not stand on the top step or cap of a stepladder, or above the highest standing level marked on an extension ladder.", "Fixed ladders with a climb of 24 feet or more need a cage, well or ladder safety system, and ladders installed or replaced since November 2018 must have a ladder safety system or personal fall arrest system.", "Ladder training under 1926.1060 is given by a competent person, covers hazards, setup, care and load capacity, and is documented on this platform."], "questions": [{"text": "A technician needs to replace a photocell on a streetlight pole next to an energized service drop. Which ladder is correct?", "options": ["A Type IA aluminum extension ladder because it is lighter", "A Type I wood ladder as long as it is dry", "A Type IA or IAA fiberglass extension ladder", "Any ladder, as long as a second worker foots it"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Near energized equipment the ladder must have nonconductive side rails, and the department uses Type IA and IAA ratings for field work. Metal ladders are never used near energized lines, and wet wood conducts too."}, {"text": "What must happen to a portable ladder found with a cracked side rail?", "options": ["Tag it 'Do Not Use' and remove it from service until repaired or discarded", "Wrap the crack with tape and finish the job", "Set it back on the truck rack and report it at the end of the week", "Keep using it for light-duty tasks only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(16) requires a ladder with structural defects to be immediately marked or tagged 'Do Not Use' and withdrawn from service until it is repaired to its original design or discarded."}, {"text": "An extension ladder is set so its top rests 20 feet up a wall. How far out from the wall should the feet be?", "options": ["2 feet", "10 feet", "8 feet", "5 feet"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The 4 to 1 rule puts the feet one quarter of the working length away from the wall. One quarter of 20 feet is 5 feet."}, {"text": "A ladder used to reach a roof is too short for the rails to extend 3 feet above the edge. What does the standard allow?", "options": ["Set the ladder on a sturdy box to gain the missing height", "Secure the ladder at the top to a rigid support and install a grab rail or grab device", "Use it as long as a worker foots the bottom", "Lean a second ladder beside it for a handhold"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(1) requires the rails to extend at least 3 feet above the landing, or the ladder must be secured at the top to a rigid support and a grab device provided. Boxes are never an acceptable base."}, {"text": "Which of these is prohibited by the standard?", "options": ["Facing the ladder while climbing", "Using a hand line to raise tools after reaching the work", "Placing a ladder on a barrel to reach a higher point", "Tying the top of the ladder off to a rigid support"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(6) requires stable and level footing, and the general industry rule in 1910.23(c) says directly that ladders are not placed on boxes, barrels or other unstable bases to gain height. The other choices are correct practices."}, {"text": "What does 'three points of contact' mean when climbing a ladder?", "options": ["Both feet on the same rung and one hand on a rail", "Two hands and one foot, or two feet and one hand, on the ladder at all times", "One hand, one foot and one tool in contact with the ladder", "Three rungs between you and the top of the ladder"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Three points of contact means two hands and one foot or two feet and one hand are always on the ladder. The standard also requires at least one hand to grasp the ladder at all times while climbing."}, {"text": "Where may a worker stand on a stepladder?", "options": ["On the top cap, as long as the spreaders are locked", "On the top step, if a second worker holds the ladder", "On any step, including the pail shelf, if the duty rating allows it", "On the steps below the top step, never on the top step or top cap"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.1053(b)(13) prohibits using the top or top step of a stepladder as a step. The cap is not a step, no matter who is holding the ladder."}, {"text": "A worker on an extension ladder needs to reach a little higher. Which choice follows the standard and the manufacturer's label?", "options": ["Climb down and get a longer ladder, since the label marks the highest standing level", "Stand on the top rung and hold the wall for balance", "Have a coworker extend the ladder while the worker stays on it", "Stand on the second rung from the top and lean out past the rail"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Workers must not stand above the highest standing level shown on the manufacturer's label, must not have a ladder moved or extended while they are on it, and must keep their body centered between the rails."}, {"text": "At what length of climb do the fixed ladder rules require a cage, well, ladder safety system or personal fall arrest system?", "options": ["10 feet", "16 feet", "24 feet", "50 feet"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Both 1926.1053(a)(19) and the general industry rule 1910.28(b)(9) use 24 feet as the trigger. Fixed ladders installed or replaced since November 2018 need a ladder safety system or PFAS rather than a cage alone."}, {"text": "Which topic must ladder training under 1926.1060 include?", "options": ["How to repair a cracked fiberglass rail in the field", "The maximum intended load-carrying capacity of the ladders used", "The purchase history of each ladder on the truck", "How to tie two ladders together to make a longer one"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.1060(a)(1)(iv) requires training on the maximum intended load-carrying capacity of ladders used, along with hazards, proper construction, use, placement and care. Field repairs and tying ladders together are prohibited practices."}]}, {"slug": "respiratory-protection", "title": "Respiratory Protection", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "standard_title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 6, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Respiratory Protection, 29 CFR 1910.134, sets the rules for selecting, fitting, using and caring for respirators whenever engineering controls cannot keep airborne hazards below safe levels. This course explains required versus voluntary use, the written program, medical evaluation, fit testing, seal checks, cartridge change schedules and IDLH atmospheres, with examples from fleet shops, concrete cutting, water and wastewater plants, sewers and older facilities. It also covers the program gaps that most often draw citations.", "objectives": ["Distinguish required respirator use from voluntary use and explain what Appendix D requires.", "Describe the elements of a written respiratory protection program and the role of the program administrator.", "Select the right type of NIOSH approved respirator, filter or cartridge for a given hazard using assigned protection factors.", "Explain why medical evaluation must come before fit testing and why fit testing is repeated at least annually.", "Perform a user seal check and identify conditions, such as facial hair, that defeat the seal.", "Recognize IDLH atmospheres and the respirators required for confined space and emergency entry."], "sections": [{"heading": "When respirators are required, and when use is voluntary", "html": "<p>Silica dust, welding fume, chlorine and sewer gas can damage your lungs or take your life, in minutes or over years. The standard puts <strong>engineering controls first</strong>: local exhaust, wet cutting and ventilation. When those cannot keep exposures below the <strong>permissible exposure limit (PEL)</strong>, the employer must provide respirators and run a full program.</p>\n<p>Respirator use falls into two categories:<br />\n- <strong>Required use.</strong> The employer has determined a respirator is necessary to protect your health. Every part of the standard applies.<br />\n- <strong>Voluntary use.</strong> Exposures are below the limits but an employee wants to wear a respirator anyway. The employer must make sure the respirator will not itself create a hazard and must give the employee <strong>Appendix D</strong>. If the voluntary respirator is more than a <strong>filtering facepiece</strong> (a disposable dust mask such as an N95), the employee also needs a medical evaluation and the respirator must be maintained under a written program.</p>\n<p>Appendix D tells voluntary users to follow the manufacturer instructions, use a NIOSH certified respirator only for what it is designed for, and never share it.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Respiratory Protection is on OSHA's most cited list every year. Inspectors rarely find a crew with no respirators. They find respirators handed out without the program behind them.</p>\n<p>The most common citations are:<br />\n- <strong>No medical evaluation.</strong> An employee was fit tested or assigned a respirator before a health care professional cleared them to wear one.<br />\n- <strong>No fit test.</strong> Tight fitting respirators were issued without an initial fit test, or the annual fit test lapsed.<br />\n- <strong>No written program.</strong> The department has respirators in the stockroom but no written program, or no one named to run it.</p>\n<p>Other frequent findings include no training, no hazard evaluation, voluntary users who never received Appendix D, and tight fitting respirators worn over beards.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written program and the administrator", "html": "<p>Wherever respirators are required, the employer must have a <strong>written respiratory protection program</strong> with worksite specific procedures, run by a trained <strong>program administrator</strong> who oversees it and evaluates how well it works.</p>\n<p>The program must cover:<br />\n- Respirator selection<br />\n- Medical evaluations<br />\n- Fit testing<br />\n- Routine and emergency use procedures<br />\n- Cleaning, storing, inspecting, repairing and discarding respirators<br />\n- Air quality for atmosphere supplying respirators<br />\n- Training in hazards and in respirator use and limitations<br />\n- Regular evaluation of the program</p>\n<p>The administrator should check with users regularly and review the program whenever work processes or chemicals change. The employer pays for required respirators, medical evaluations, fit tests and training.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hazard evaluation and respirator selection", "html": "<p>The employer must <strong>identify and evaluate</strong> the hazard before choosing a respirator: measure or estimate the exposure, identify the contaminant and its form, and check for oxygen deficiency. If exposure cannot be reasonably estimated, the atmosphere is treated as <strong>IDLH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Only <strong>NIOSH approved</strong> respirators may be used. There are two families:<br />\n- <strong>Air purifying respirators (APR)</strong> clean the air through filters or cartridges. They never add oxygen, so they cannot be used where oxygen is below 19.5 percent or in IDLH atmospheres.<br />\n- <strong>Atmosphere supplying respirators</strong> deliver clean air from a tank or compressor: SCBA and supplied air respirators.</p>\n<p><strong>Particulate filters</strong> are rated by letter and number. <strong>N</strong> means not oil resistant, <strong>R</strong> oil resistant and <strong>P</strong> oil proof. The number is the minimum efficiency: 95, 99 or 99.97 percent (labeled 100). An <strong>N95</strong> handles most dusts. A <strong>P100</strong> is the choice for silica, lead, asbestos and welding fume. <strong>Chemical cartridges</strong> are color coded by contaminant. Match the cartridge to the SDS.</p>\n<p>The <strong>assigned protection factor (APF)</strong> is how much a properly fitted respirator reduces exposure. A half mask APR has an APF of 10, a full facepiece APR 50 and a pressure demand SCBA 10,000. Pick a respirator with an APF high enough for the measured exposure.</p>"}, {"heading": "Medical evaluation, fit testing and the seal", "html": "<p>Wearing a respirator is work for your heart and lungs. Before anyone is fit tested or wears a required respirator, they must have a <strong>medical evaluation</strong>. It begins with the Appendix C questionnaire, reviewed by a physician or other licensed health care professional. The employer is told only whether the person can wear the respirator and under what limits.</p>\n<p>After medical clearance comes the <strong>fit test</strong>. Every tight fitting respirator, including N95 filtering facepieces, must be fit tested before first use, at least <strong>every 12 months</strong>, whenever a different make, model or size is used, and whenever a change in the face could affect the fit. <strong>Qualitative</strong> fit testing uses a test agent such as Bitrex or saccharin; you pass if you cannot taste or smell it. <strong>Quantitative</strong> fit testing uses an instrument to measure leakage.</p>\n<p>Fit testing proves the respirator can seal. The <strong>user seal check</strong> proves it did seal <strong>this time</strong>. Do one every time you put a tight fitting respirator on. Positive pressure: cover the exhalation valve and breathe out gently; the facepiece should bulge with no leaks. Negative pressure: cover the inlets and breathe in; the facepiece should collapse and stay collapsed.</p>\n<p><strong>Facial hair</strong> between the sealing surface and the skin is not allowed with a tight fitting respirator. Stubble is enough to break the seal. Employees who cannot shave need a loose fitting PAPR hood or helmet.</p>"}, {"heading": "Care, cleaning, storage and cartridge changes", "html": "<p><strong>Clean and disinfect</strong> reusable respirators as often as needed to keep them sanitary: after each day for one user, after each use when shared, and after every fit test.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspect</strong> before each use and during cleaning for cracks, tears, stiff valves and missing parts. Emergency respirators and SCBA are inspected monthly.</p>\n<p><strong>Store</strong> respirators away from damage, contamination, dust, sunlight, extreme temperatures and moisture. A sealed bag in a clean locker works. A dusty dashboard does not.</p>\n<p>Chemical cartridges do not last forever, and smell is not a reliable warning. The employer must set a <strong>change schedule</strong> based on objective information, such as manufacturer service life data and the measured exposure, or use cartridges with an <strong>end of service life indicator (ESLI)</strong>. Replace particulate filters when breathing becomes harder.</p>\n<p>Employees must be allowed to leave the work area to wash the facepiece, replace a cartridge or filter, or whenever they smell or taste the contaminant.</p>"}, {"heading": "IDLH atmospheres and confined spaces", "html": "<p>An atmosphere <strong>immediately dangerous to life or health (IDLH)</strong> could kill, permanently harm or disable your ability to escape. Oxygen below 19.5 percent is IDLH. So is a manhole with hydrogen sulfide, a chlorine room after a leak, or any space where the hazard is unknown.</p>\n<p>IDLH entry requires a <strong>full facepiece pressure demand SCBA</strong> or a pressure demand supplied air respirator with an auxiliary self contained air supply. At least one trained standby person must be outside, in contact with the entrant, equipped with the same respirator and retrieval gear, and must call for help before entering to rescue.</p>\n<p>For Public Works the IDLH rule meets the confined space rule most often in <strong>wastewater and stormwater work</strong>: manholes, wet wells, lift stations and vaults. Hydrogen sulfide deadens the sense of smell at dangerous levels, and methane displaces oxygen. Air purifying respirators, including cartridge masks sold for sewer gas, are <strong>never</strong> acceptable for entry into an atmosphere that is or may be IDLH. Test the air before and during entry, ventilate, and follow the confined space program. Operators who respond to a chlorine leak need SCBA, not a cartridge respirator, because leak concentrations are unknown.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> The fleet shop is where most required respirator use happens. <strong>Welding fume</strong> on galvanized or painted metal, <strong>sandblasting</strong> and spray painting all call for an exposure evaluation and often a respirator. Streets and sign shop crews who <strong>cut, grind or core concrete or asphalt</strong> are exposed to <strong>respirable crystalline silica</strong>. Wet cutting and vacuum shrouds come first. Facilities staff disturbing insulation or floor tile in <strong>older buildings</strong> must assume <strong>asbestos</strong> until a survey proves otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater plants face <strong>chlorine</strong>, sulfur dioxide and ammonia in daily work and SCBA level hazards during leaks. Collections crews face <strong>sewer gases</strong> in manholes and lift stations. Forestry crews applying <strong>pesticides and herbicides</strong> must use the respirator shown on the EPA product label.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line and substation crews meet respirator hazards less often: cutting concrete pads and vault lids, battery room acid mist, lead paint on older structures and SF6 by-products after a breaker fault. Underground vaults carry the same gas hazards as a manhole.</p>\n<p>In every division the sequence is the same: evaluate the hazard, control it at the source, then choose a respirator. Medical clearance comes before fit testing, fit testing before use, and a seal check before every use.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Engineering controls come first, and respirators are required only when those controls cannot keep exposures below the permissible exposure limit.", "Voluntary users of filtering facepieces must receive the Appendix D information, and voluntary users of any other respirator also need a medical evaluation and basic maintenance rules.", "A written, worksite specific program run by a trained program administrator is required wherever respirators are required.", "Only NIOSH approved respirators may be used, matched to the hazard with the right filter or cartridge and an assigned protection factor high enough for the exposure.", "Medical evaluation comes before fit testing, and fit testing comes before first use and at least every 12 months after that.", "A user seal check is done every time a tight fitting respirator is put on, and facial hair in the seal area is not allowed.", "Air purifying respirators never add oxygen and are never used in IDLH atmospheres, including manholes, wet wells and chlorine leaks, where SCBA or supplied air with escape bottle is required.", "The most common citations, no medical evaluation, no fit test and no written program, all come from handing out respirators without the program behind them."], "questions": [{"text": "An employee wants to wear a disposable N95 while sweeping the shop, even though exposure is below the permissible limit. What must the employer do?", "options": ["Nothing, because voluntary use is outside the standard", "Provide the Appendix D information and make sure the respirator will not itself create a hazard", "Require a quantitative fit test before the employee can wear it", "Prohibit the use, because respirators are allowed only when required"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Voluntary use of a filtering facepiece requires the employer to give the employee Appendix D and to confirm the respirator will not create a hazard. Medical evaluation and a written program are required for voluntary use only when the respirator is more than a filtering facepiece."}, {"text": "What is the correct order of steps before an employee uses a tight fitting respirator for the first time?", "options": ["Fit test, then medical evaluation, then user seal check", "User seal check, then fit test, then medical evaluation", "Medical evaluation, then fit test, then user seal check each time it is worn", "Medical evaluation and fit test are both optional if the employee has worn respirators before"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires medical clearance before fit testing, fit testing before first use, and a user seal check every time the respirator is put on."}, {"text": "How often must fit testing be repeated for an employee who continues to use the same respirator?", "options": ["At least every 12 months", "Every 6 months", "Every 2 years", "Only when the employee changes jobs"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Fit testing is required before first use, at least annually, and whenever the respirator model or size changes or a change in the wearer's face could affect the fit."}, {"text": "Which filter is the right choice for respirable crystalline silica, lead or asbestos work where the highest level of particulate filtration is needed?", "options": ["N95", "R95", "Organic vapor cartridge", "P100"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A P100 filter is the most efficient particulate class (99.97 percent) and is oil proof, making it the standard choice for the most toxic particulates. An organic vapor cartridge does not filter particles."}, {"text": "A collections crew needs to enter a manhole where the gas meter reads 17 percent oxygen. What respirator is required?", "options": ["A half mask with acid gas cartridges", "A full facepiece air purifying respirator with P100 filters", "A full facepiece pressure demand SCBA, or supplied air with an auxiliary escape cylinder", "An N95 filtering facepiece with a user seal check"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Oxygen below 19.5 percent is IDLH. Air purifying respirators cannot add oxygen, so IDLH entry requires pressure demand SCBA or a pressure demand supplied air respirator with an auxiliary self contained air supply."}, {"text": "What is the assigned protection factor of a properly fitted half mask air purifying respirator?", "options": ["5", "10", "50", "1,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A half mask APR has an APF of 10, meaning it should reduce exposure to one tenth of the airborne concentration when properly fitted and used. A full facepiece APR has an APF of 50."}, {"text": "Which statement about facial hair and respirators is correct?", "options": ["A short, neatly trimmed beard is acceptable under a half mask if the fit test is passed", "Facial hair matters only for full facepiece respirators", "Facial hair is acceptable as long as the employee does a user seal check", "Facial hair that comes between the sealing surface and the face is not allowed with any tight fitting respirator"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standard prohibits tight fitting respirators on employees with facial hair that interferes with the seal or the valves. Employees who cannot shave need a loose fitting hood or helmet style PAPR."}, {"text": "What must a cartridge change schedule be based on?", "options": ["The user's sense of smell or taste for the contaminant", "Objective information such as manufacturer service life data and measured exposure, or an end of service life indicator", "A fixed rule of one new cartridge per month", "Whether the cartridge looks dirty"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Smell is not a reliable warning for many chemicals, so the employer must base the change schedule on objective data or use cartridges with an end of service life indicator."}, {"text": "Who is told the full results of an employee's respirator medical evaluation?", "options": ["The employee, who receives a written recommendation, while the employer is told only whether the employee can wear the respirator and any limits", "The supervisor, who keeps a copy in the employee's personnel file", "The program administrator and the safety committee", "The employer, who must post the results with the written program"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The health care professional gives the employee a written recommendation and tells the employer only whether the employee is medically able to use the respirator and under what limits. The medical details stay confidential."}, {"text": "A streets crew is dry cutting a concrete curb with a gas powered saw. What should happen first under the respiratory protection approach?", "options": ["Issue N95 respirators to the crew and continue dry cutting", "Issue P100 respirators without a fit test because the job is short", "Switch to wet cutting or a vacuum shroud to control the silica dust at the source, then evaluate whether a respirator is still needed", "Have the crew hold their breath while making the cut"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires engineering controls such as wet cutting or dust collection first. A respirator is selected only after the remaining exposure has been evaluated, and then only after medical clearance and fit testing."}]}, {"slug": "powered-industrial-trucks", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (Forklifts)", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "standard_title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 7, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 35, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Forklifts, telehandlers and powered pallet jacks move heavy loads in public works warehouses, parts rooms, material yards and plants, and they tip over, strike pedestrians and drop loads when operated carelessly. This course covers the training, evaluation and certification rules in 29 CFR 1910.178, daily inspections, the stability triangle and load handling, safe travel and parking, and refueling and charging. The content supports the hands-on training and workplace evaluation that every operator must complete.", "objectives": ["Identify which equipment counts as a powered industrial truck and which does not.", "Describe the three parts of operator training and when refresher training and reevaluation are required.", "Perform a pre-operation inspection and explain why a defective truck is removed from service.", "Explain the stability triangle, load center and capacity plate, and how they relate to tipovers.", "Apply safe practices for traveling, pedestrians, ramps, blind corners, raising people and parking.", "Follow the rules for refueling, propane cylinder handling and battery charging areas."], "sections": [{"heading": "What counts as a powered industrial truck", "html": "<p>29 CFR 1910.178 covers <strong>powered industrial trucks</strong>: mobile, power-propelled trucks used to carry, push, pull, lift, stack or tier material. In public works that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counterbalanced forklifts</strong>, sit-down and stand-up, electric or engine powered.</li>\n<li><strong>Rough terrain forklifts</strong> and <strong>telehandlers</strong> used in material yards and at plants.</li>\n<li><strong>Powered pallet jacks</strong> and walkie stackers, whether ridden or walked behind.</li>\n<li><strong>Order pickers</strong> and other trucks that lift the operator with the load.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The standard does <strong>not</strong> cover vehicles intended for earth moving or over-the-road hauling. Loaders, backhoes, skid steers and dump trucks fall under other rules even when they happen to move a pallet. Manual pallet jacks with no motor are also outside the standard.</p>\n<p>Every truck has a <strong>designation</strong> (such as LP, LPS, E, ES, EE, EX, G, GS, D or DS) based on its power source and built-in safeguards. Only trucks with the right designation may operate in hazardous locations where flammable gases, vapors or dusts may be present, such as digester and gas handling areas at treatment plants.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Powered industrial trucks appear on OSHA's most cited list every year, and the reasons are consistent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operators not trained or evaluated.</strong> People who drive a forklift a few times a month are often never formally trained, or were trained years ago and never reevaluated.</li>\n<li><strong>No certification records.</strong> Training happened, but no record shows the operator's name, the training and evaluation dates and who conducted them.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing refresher training</strong> after an accident, a near miss or an observed unsafe act.</li>\n<li><strong>Defective trucks kept in service.</strong> Worn forks, dead horns, bad brakes, leaking hydraulics and missing seat belts on trucks still in daily use.</li>\n<li><strong>No daily inspection.</strong> Trucks are started and driven without anyone checking them.</li>\n<li><strong>Unsafe operation.</strong> Speeding, carrying loads high, riders on the forks and forks left raised when parked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works departments are exposed because forklift use is often occasional, spread across many sites and done by staff whose main job is something else.</p>"}, {"heading": "Operator training, evaluation and certification", "html": "<p>No one may operate a powered industrial truck until they have completed training and been evaluated. Trainees may operate only under the direct supervision of a trained, experienced person and where it does not endanger others.</p>\n<p>Training has <strong>three required parts</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formal instruction</strong>, such as classroom time, videos, written material or online modules like this course.</li>\n<li><strong>Practical training</strong>, where a trainer demonstrates and the operator performs exercises on the actual type of truck.</li>\n<li><strong>An evaluation of the operator's performance in the workplace</strong>, handling real loads in the real environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training covers truck-related topics (controls, capacity, stability, inspection, refueling) and workplace-related topics (surfaces, ramps, pedestrians, narrow aisles, hazardous locations and the loads handled), and it must match the truck types the operator will use.</p>\n<p>Each operator's performance must be <strong>evaluated at least once every three years</strong>. <strong>Refresher training</strong> and a new evaluation are required sooner when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the operator is involved in an accident or near miss,</li>\n<li>the operator is observed operating unsafely,</li>\n<li>an evaluation shows the operator is not driving safely,</li>\n<li>the operator is assigned to a different type of truck, or</li>\n<li>workplace conditions change in a way that affects safe operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The employer must <strong>certify</strong> that each operator has been trained and evaluated. The record includes the operator's name, the date of training, the date of evaluation and the identity of the person who did the training or evaluation. Under federal child labor rules, operators must be at least <strong>18 years old</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "Pre-operation inspection", "html": "<p>The standard requires every truck to be examined <strong>before it is placed in service and at least daily</strong>, and after each shift when it runs around the clock. In practice, check it before every shift using the checklist for the truck type. A typical walkaround covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>tires, wheels and lug nuts,</li>\n<li>forks for cracks, bends, wear at the heel and a secure locking pin,</li>\n<li>mast, chains and hoses for damage and leaks,</li>\n<li>fluid levels, and fuel or battery condition,</li>\n<li>overhead guard, load backrest and seat belt,</li>\n<li>data plate present and readable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then a seated check with the truck running: horn, lights, backup alarm, brakes, parking brake, steering, lift and tilt controls, and gauges.</p>\n<p>If any defect affects safety, the truck is <strong>taken out of service</strong> and tagged until it is repaired. A truck that is defective or in any way unsafe does not run, and repairs are done only by authorized personnel.</p>"}, {"heading": "Stability, load center and capacity", "html": "<p>A forklift stays upright as long as its combined center of gravity stays inside the <strong>stability triangle</strong>, the area between the two front wheels and the pivot point of the rear axle. Lifting a load, tilting the mast forward, turning sharply or driving on a slope all move the center of gravity toward the edge of that triangle. Push it outside and the truck tips.</p>\n<p>The <strong>capacity plate</strong> (data plate) on every truck states how much it can lift at a rated <strong>load center</strong>, usually 24 inches from the face of the forks, at a stated height. Capacity drops when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the load center is farther out because the load is long or sits away from the backrest,</li>\n<li>the mast is raised high or tilted forward,</li>\n<li>attachments such as a drum clamp, jib or rotator are added.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Never exceed the plate, and never add counterweight to lift more. Modifications and attachments need the manufacturer's approval and an updated plate.</p>\n<p>Handle loads this way:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Center the load, seat it against the backrest and tilt the mast back slightly.</li>\n<li>Travel with the forks low, about 4 to 6 inches off the floor.</li>\n<li>Raise and lower only when stopped.</li>\n<li>Avoid sudden starts, stops and turns, especially with the load raised.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Traveling, pedestrians, ramps and raising people", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Speed.</strong> Drive at a speed that lets you stop safely, and slow down on wet or uneven surfaces and in congested areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Pedestrians.</strong> Pedestrians have the right of way. Keep a safe distance, make eye contact and never let anyone walk or stand under raised forks.</li>\n<li><strong>Blind corners, doorways and aisle crossings.</strong> Slow down, <strong>sound the horn</strong> and look in the direction of travel.</li>\n<li><strong>Visibility.</strong> If the load blocks your view, travel in reverse with the load trailing, except when going up a ramp.</li>\n<li><strong>Ramps and grades.</strong> With a load, drive <strong>with the load upgrade</strong>: forward going up and in reverse going down. Travel straight up and down, never turn on a grade, and keep the forks low with the mast tilted back.</li>\n<li><strong>Seat belt.</strong> Wear it every time on a sit-down truck. In a tipover, stay in the seat, hold the wheel, brace your feet and lean away from the fall. Jumping is how operators get crushed.</li>\n<li><strong>Riders and raising people.</strong> No passengers. Never lift a person on the forks, a pallet or a bucket. People may be elevated only on a <strong>manufacturer-approved work platform</strong> secured to the forks, with the operator at the controls, fall protection for the person on the platform and the truck not moving.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"heading": "Refueling, propane, charging and parking", "html": "<p><strong>Refueling and charging areas</strong> must be set up for the purpose: adequate ventilation, no open flames or smoking, fire protection nearby and spill control. Engines are shut off during refueling.</p>\n<p><strong>Propane (LP) cylinders.</strong> Close the cylinder valve before changing cylinders. Wear eye protection and gloves because escaping propane can cause frostbite. Check the O-ring and connections, and seat the cylinder with the relief valve pointing up. Store spare cylinders outdoors or in an approved cage, away from exits and heat.</p>\n<p><strong>Battery charging.</strong> Park, set the brake and turn the truck off before connecting the charger. Keep the vent caps in place, charge with the compartment open, and keep sparks and flames away because charging produces hydrogen gas. Know where the eyewash is before you need it.</p>\n<p><strong>Parking an unattended truck</strong> means forks fully lowered, controls in neutral, parking brake set and power shut off. A truck is unattended when the operator is 25 feet or more away, or whenever the operator cannot see it. Even within 25 feet and in view, the load goes down, the controls go to neutral and the brake is set. Never park where you block an aisle, exit, fire equipment or electrical panel.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Transformers, cable reels, pole hardware and meter stock are heavy, awkward loads. Use the right attachment for reels, verify the transformer weight against the capacity plate at the real load center, and keep the load low on the way out of the yard. Yard surfaces are often gravel and uneven, so slow down.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Telehandlers at treatment plants move chemical totes, pump assemblies and spare parts across rough ground and into tight pump rooms. Confirm the truck's designation is allowed in any area with methane or flammable vapors. Powered pallet jacks at the scale house and plant storerooms are covered too, including the training rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> The central warehouse and parts rooms see the most forklift traffic, often mixed with walk-in customers from other divisions. Mark pedestrian lanes, sound the horn at aisle ends and keep forks down when staged. Sign shops handle long bundles of posts and sheeting that push the load center out. Facilities crews using powered pallet jacks at community buildings still need training, inspection and a certification record.</p>\n<p>Across all three divisions: if you were never trained and evaluated on that type of truck, you do not drive it.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Powered industrial trucks include forklifts, telehandlers, powered pallet jacks and order pickers, but not earth moving equipment or manual pallet jacks.", "No one operates a truck without formal instruction, practical training and a workplace evaluation, and operators must be at least 18.", "Operators are reevaluated at least every three years, and sooner after an accident, near miss, unsafe operation, a change in truck type or a change in conditions.", "The employer keeps a certification record with the operator's name, training date, evaluation date and the trainer or evaluator's identity.", "Inspect the truck before every shift and take any truck with a safety defect out of service until it is repaired.", "Stay inside the stability triangle: respect the capacity plate and load center, keep the load low and tilted back, and drive with the load upgrade on ramps.", "Wear the seat belt, sound the horn at blind corners, give pedestrians the right of way and never lift people without an approved platform.", "Park with forks down, controls in neutral, brake set and power off, and handle propane and battery charging only in areas set up for it."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of these is covered by 29 CFR 1910.178?", "options": ["A skid steer loader moving a pallet of salt", "A manual pallet jack in the parts room", "A battery-powered walkie pallet jack at the facilities storeroom", "A dump truck hauling road gravel"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Powered pallet jacks are powered industrial trucks and fall under the standard. Earth moving equipment, over-the-road trucks and manual pallet jacks do not."}, {"text": "What are the three required parts of powered industrial truck operator training?", "options": ["Formal instruction, practical training and an evaluation of performance in the workplace", "A written test, a driving test at a training center and a license from the state", "Watching a video, signing an acknowledgment and completing a probation period", "Classroom time, a physical exam and a supervisor's recommendation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires formal instruction, practical training on the truck and an evaluation of the operator's performance in the actual workplace before operating without supervision."}, {"text": "How often must an operator's performance be evaluated if no accidents or changes occur?", "options": ["Every year", "At least once every three years", "Every five years", "Only when first hired"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard requires an evaluation of each operator's performance at least once every three years, with refresher training sooner when specific events occur."}, {"text": "An operator has a near miss when a load slides off the forks. What does the standard require?", "options": ["A written warning placed in the personnel file", "A new state operator's license", "Nothing, as long as no one was hurt", "Refresher training and a new evaluation"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An accident or near miss is one of the events that triggers refresher training and reevaluation, along with unsafe operation, a new truck type and changed workplace conditions."}, {"text": "During the pre-shift check, the horn on a forklift does not work. What should the operator do?", "options": ["Take the truck out of service and tag it until the horn is repaired", "Drive slowly and shout at blind corners until the next scheduled service", "Use the truck only in the yard where there are fewer pedestrians", "Note the problem on the checklist and continue the shift"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Any defect that affects safe operation means the truck is removed from service until it is repaired. The horn is required for warning pedestrians at blind corners and doorways."}, {"text": "Which action moves a forklift's center of gravity toward the edge of the stability triangle?", "options": ["Lowering the forks to travel height", "Tilting the mast back with the load seated against the backrest", "Raising a load high and tilting the mast forward", "Driving slowly in a straight line on level ground"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Raising the load and tilting forward shift the combined center of gravity up and forward, toward the front edge of the triangle, where the truck can tip forward."}, {"text": "You are driving a loaded forklift down a ramp. How should you travel?", "options": ["Forward, with the forks raised to clear the slope", "In reverse, with the load upgrade and forks low", "Forward, turning slightly to slow the descent", "In reverse, with the mast tilted forward"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "On a grade the load always points upgrade. Going down, that means backing down with the forks low and the mast tilted back."}, {"text": "A coworker needs to reach a light fixture 12 feet up. What is the only acceptable way to lift them with a forklift?", "options": ["Standing on a pallet strapped to the forks", "Standing on the forks while holding the mast", "Sitting in a bucket chained to the carriage", "On a manufacturer-approved work platform secured to the forks, with the operator at the controls"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "People may be elevated only on an approved work platform that is secured to the truck, with the operator staying at the controls and the person on the platform protected from falling."}, {"text": "An operator walks 40 feet from a forklift to check a parts bin. What is required before leaving the truck?", "options": ["Only that the engine is left idling so it restarts easily", "Forks lowered, controls in neutral, parking brake set and power shut off", "Forks raised to keep them off the floor and the horn tapped twice", "The key left in the ignition so a coworker can move the truck"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A truck is unattended when the operator is 25 feet or more away or out of sight of it. An unattended truck must have the forks fully lowered, controls neutral, brake set and power off."}, {"text": "Which item must be in the employer's operator certification record?", "options": ["The operator's driver's license number", "The serial number of each truck the operator has driven", "The operator's insurance carrier", "The identity of the person who performed the training or evaluation"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The certification must include the operator's name, the training date, the evaluation date and the identity of the person who conducted the training or evaluation."}]}, {"slug": "fall-protection-training", "title": "Fall Protection: Training Requirements", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "standard_title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 8, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 25, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Owning fall protection equipment is not enough. OSHA requires every worker who might be exposed to a fall hazard to be trained by a competent person, and it requires the employer to prove that training happened with a written record. This course explains who must be trained, what the training must cover, when retraining is required, and how this platform and your hands-on sessions together create the record OSHA expects to see.", "objectives": ["State who must receive fall protection training under 1926.503 and what the training program must enable workers to do.", "List the subject areas the training must cover, from recognizing fall hazards to the correct use of each protection system.", "Explain the difference between a competent person and a qualified person and describe the role each plays in fall protection.", "Identify the three items that must appear on the written training certification record.", "Recognize the three situations that require retraining and apply them to real changes on a public works crew.", "Describe how this course, the platform record and your hands-on session together document compliance."], "sections": [{"heading": "Who must be trained", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(a)(1)</strong> requires the employer to provide a training program for <strong>each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards</strong>. The word is 'might.' You do not have to work at height every day to be covered. If your job could put you on a roof, a bridge deck, a tank top, an open excavation edge or an aerial lift even once a year, you must be trained before that exposure happens.</p>\n<p>The program has two goals. It must enable each employee to <strong>recognize the hazards of falling</strong>, and it must train each employee in the <strong>procedures to follow to minimize those hazards</strong>. Knowing that a harness exists is not the same as knowing when it is required, how to inspect it, where to anchor it and how far you will fall if it has to work.</p>\n<p>Our department renews this course every 12 months so the record never goes stale.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>The training standard usually gets cited in the same inspection as the general requirements standard, 1926.501. An inspector sees a worker on an edge without protection, asks when fall protection is required and how the equipment is used, and gets a vague answer. Then the inspector asks for the training record. There is none, or it is years old and does not cover the equipment in use.</p>\n<p>The paragraphs cited most often are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(a)(1)</strong> No training program at all for exposed workers.</li>\n<li><strong>(b)(1)</strong> No written certification record, or a record missing the name, date or signature.</li>\n<li><strong>(c)</strong> Failure to retrain after a change in equipment or after a worker showed they did not understand the system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fix is a program that covers the required topics, delivered by a competent person, with a dated and signed record for every exposed worker. That is what this course and your hands-on session are built to produce.</p>"}, {"heading": "What the training must cover", "html": "<p>Under <strong>1926.503(a)(2)</strong>, each employee must be trained, as needed for their work, in these areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>nature of fall hazards</strong> in the work area: open edges, holes, leading edges, excavations, roofs, tanks and structures.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for <strong>erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting</strong> the fall protection systems to be used.</li>\n<li>The <strong>use and operation</strong> of guardrail systems, personal fall arrest systems, safety net systems, warning line systems, safety monitoring systems, controlled access zones and any other protection to be used.</li>\n<li>Each employee's <strong>role in a safety monitoring system</strong>, when one is used.</li>\n<li>Limits on using mechanical equipment during roofing work on low-slope roofs.</li>\n<li>Correct procedures for the <strong>handling and storage of equipment and materials</strong> and for the <strong>erection of overhead protection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each employee's <strong>role in a fall protection plan</strong>, when a plan is used instead of conventional systems.</li>\n<li>The standards in <strong>Subpart M</strong> itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The phrase 'as needed' matters. A fleet mechanic who only uses the shop's overhead fall arrest rail does not need to know how to rig a safety net. A bridge crew that erects temporary guardrails does. Training has to match the systems your crew actually uses, which is why the hands-on portion is done by division.</p>"}, {"heading": "Competent person and qualified person", "html": "<p>OSHA's construction standards use two terms that are often mixed up. The difference affects who can train you and who can design your anchor.</p>\n<p>A <strong>competent person</strong> is someone who can identify existing and predictable fall hazards in the work area <strong>and</strong> has the <strong>authority to take prompt corrective action</strong> to eliminate them. Both parts are required. A crew member who can spot a missing guardrail but cannot stop the job is not a competent person. The standard requires fall protection training to be given <strong>by a competent person qualified in the subject areas</strong> being taught. The competent person also inspects fall arrest equipment that has caught a fall before it returns to service, serves as the monitor in a safety monitoring system, and supervises the implementation of a fall protection plan where one is used.</p>\n<p>A <strong>qualified person</strong> has a recognized degree, certificate or professional standing, or extensive knowledge, training and experience, and has demonstrated the ability to solve problems in the subject. A qualified person designs engineered anchorages that do not meet the 5,000 pound rule on their own, certifies horizontal lifeline systems and prepares fall protection plans.</p>\n<p>One person can hold both roles, but the roles come from knowledge and authority, not job title. Your division safety lead will tell you who serves as competent person on your crew.</p>"}, {"heading": "The written certification record", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(b)</strong> requires the employer to verify training with a <strong>written certification record</strong>. The record must contain three things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>name or other identity</strong> of the employee trained.</li>\n<li>The <strong>date or dates</strong> of the training.</li>\n<li>The <strong>signature</strong> of the person who conducted the training, or the signature of the employer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the employer relies on training a worker received from a previous employer, the record must show the date the employer <strong>determined that prior training was adequate</strong>. A new hire's old wallet card is not a record until a competent person here has reviewed what it covered and signed off.</p>\n<p>The employer must keep the <strong>latest</strong> certification. A record that cannot be found is treated the same as training that never happened.</p>"}, {"heading": "When retraining is required", "html": "<p>Section <strong>1926.503(c)</strong> requires retraining whenever the employer has reason to believe a trained worker <strong>does not have the understanding and skill</strong> the standard requires. The standard lists three situations, and the list is not meant to be complete:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Changes in the workplace</strong> make previous training obsolete. Examples: the plant adds a new elevated walkway, a crew is assigned to a bridge project for the first time, or a facility gets a new roof hatch and skylights.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes in the types of fall protection systems or equipment</strong> make previous training obsolete. Examples: the division switches from shock-absorbing lanyards to self-retracting lifelines, buys a new style of harness, or installs a horizontal lifeline on the water tower.</li>\n<li><strong>Inadequacies in a worker's knowledge or use</strong> of fall protection show the worker has not retained the understanding or skill. Examples: a supervisor sees someone tied off to a guardrail, wearing a harness with loose leg straps, or working an edge with the gear still in the truck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Retraining is not punishment. It means the system has a gap, and the fix is more training, documented with a new dated and signed record.</p>"}, {"heading": "How this course and the annual platform satisfy the record", "html": "<p>This platform records your name, the date you completed each course, your quiz score and the attestation of the department safety officer who serves as the employer's signature. That gives the three items 1926.503(b) requires, kept as the latest record and available to an inspector on request.</p>\n<p>Online training alone does not finish the job. OSHA has said in interpretation letters that computer-based training can be part of a program, but it must be paired with <strong>hands-on practice</strong> and a chance to <strong>ask a trainer questions</strong>. The hands-on portion is where you put on a harness, inspect a lanyard, set up the guardrails your crew uses, and show your competent person you can do it. Your division schedules that session, and its date and the trainer's signature are entered on your record next to this course.</p>\n<p>If you change divisions, start using a different system, or your supervisor sees a problem, you will be assigned this course again along with a new hands-on session. If you are handed equipment you have not been trained on, say so before you use it.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line crews and substation technicians are trained on bucket truck fall protection and pole climbing under 1910.269 and Subpart V, and that covers much of what 1926.503 requires. The gap is usually in work that does not feel like line work: meter technicians on commercial rooftops and substation crews on steel during a construction project. Those exposures need to show up on the same record.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Plant operators, collection crews and solid waste staff rotate through many sites and systems: tank hatches, clarifier bridges, vault entries and transfer station edges. When a crew is assigned to a facility it has not worked before, that is a change in the workplace. Ask your supervisor whether retraining is needed before the first shift, not after.</p>\n<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Streets and bridge crews erect and take down temporary guardrails, so their training must include erecting, maintaining and disassembling those systems, not just using them. Signal technicians and forestry crews tie off in buckets. Facilities staff on roofs need to know warning lines and roof anchor use. Fleet mechanics who use the shop fall arrest rail need the retraining trigger explained: a new trolley or lanyard type means a new session.</p>\n<p>In every division, the supervisor's job is to notice the triggers and request retraining. The worker's job is to speak up when a system is unfamiliar. The record is what proves both jobs were done.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Every employee who might be exposed to a fall hazard must be trained before that exposure, not just workers who are at height every day.", "Training must enable workers to recognize fall hazards and to follow the procedures that minimize them, including erecting, inspecting and using the systems their crew actually uses.", "Fall protection training must be delivered by a competent person who is qualified in the subject areas being taught.", "A competent person can identify hazards and has authority to correct them; a qualified person has the credentials or experience to design and engineer solutions.", "The written certification record must contain the employee's name, the training date and the signature of the trainer or employer, and the latest record must be kept.", "Retraining is required when the workplace changes, when the types of systems or equipment change, or when a worker shows they have not retained the knowledge or skill.", "This platform supplies the written record, and the hands-on session with your competent person supplies the practice and the chance to ask questions."], "questions": [{"text": "Under 1926.503, which employees must receive fall protection training?", "options": ["Only employees who work at height on a daily basis", "Each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards", "Only supervisors and designated competent persons", "Only employees who use personal fall arrest systems"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard covers each employee who might be exposed to fall hazards. Occasional exposure counts, and the training must happen before the exposure."}, {"text": "What must the training program enable each employee to do?", "options": ["Design anchorages that meet the 5,000 pound requirement", "Write the department's fall protection plan", "Pass a written test on Subpart M once every five years", "Recognize fall hazards and follow the procedures that minimize them"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(a)(1) states the program must enable workers to recognize the hazards of falling and train them in the procedures to follow to minimize those hazards."}, {"text": "Which of the following is a required training topic under 1926.503(a)(2)?", "options": ["Correct procedures for erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting the fall protection systems to be used", "The purchase price and warranty terms of fall protection equipment", "How to file an OSHA complaint about a contractor", "The history of the Subpart M rulemaking"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard specifically lists erecting, maintaining, disassembling and inspecting the systems to be used, along with the nature of fall hazards and the use and operation of each type of system."}, {"text": "Who is permitted to conduct fall protection training under the standard?", "options": ["Any employee with at least one year of field experience", "The equipment vendor's sales representative", "A competent person qualified in the subject areas being taught", "Any licensed professional engineer"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(a)(2) requires training by a competent person qualified in the areas covered. Experience or a license alone does not meet that requirement."}, {"text": "Which three items must appear on the written certification record?", "options": ["Employee name, job title and division", "Employee name or identity, training date, and signature of the trainer or employer", "Training date, quiz score and equipment serial numbers", "Trainer name, course length and location of training"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(b)(1) requires the name or other identity of the employee, the date or dates of training, and the signature of the person who conducted the training or of the employer."}, {"text": "Which training certification must the employer maintain?", "options": ["The latest certification for each employee", "Every certification ever issued, going back to hire date", "Only certifications issued within the last 30 days", "Only certifications for employees who use personal fall arrest systems"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The standard requires the latest training certification to be maintained. Older records may be kept, but the current one is what must be available."}, {"text": "A division replaces its shock-absorbing lanyards with self-retracting lifelines. What does the standard require?", "options": ["Nothing, because both devices connect to the same harness D-ring", "A note in the equipment log but no training change", "Retraining only for workers who ask for it", "Retraining, because a change in the type of equipment makes previous training obsolete"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(c)(2) lists changes in the types of fall protection systems or equipment as a retraining trigger. Self-retracting lifelines have different inspection, anchoring and clearance rules than lanyards."}, {"text": "A supervisor sees a trained worker tied off to a guardrail. What does the standard call for?", "options": ["A written warning in the personnel file", "Removing the worker from fall protection duties permanently", "Retraining, because the worker has shown an inadequacy in knowledge or use of the system", "Replacing the guardrail with a stronger one"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Section 1926.503(c)(3) requires retraining when a worker's knowledge or use of fall protection shows they have not retained the required understanding or skill. A guardrail is not an approved anchorage."}, {"text": "What is the key difference between a competent person and a qualified person?", "options": ["A competent person can identify fall hazards and has authority to correct them; a qualified person has the credentials or extensive experience to design and engineer solutions", "A competent person must be a professional engineer; a qualified person must be a supervisor", "A qualified person conducts training; a competent person only inspects harnesses", "There is no difference; OSHA uses the terms interchangeably"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The competent person's role rests on hazard recognition plus authority to act. The qualified person's role rests on credentials or demonstrated expertise, such as designing an engineered anchorage."}, {"text": "According to the course, how do this platform and your hands-on session together satisfy the training requirement?", "options": ["Completing the online quiz alone satisfies every part of the standard", "The hands-on session replaces the need for any written record", "The platform records name, date and the employer's attestation, and the hands-on session with a competent person adds practice and the chance to ask questions", "The platform record is only valid if printed and kept in the truck"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The platform supplies the three items the written record requires, while the hands-on session supplies the practice and trainer access that OSHA expects to accompany computer-based training."}]}, {"slug": "eye-face-protection", "title": "Eye and Face Protection", "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "standard_title": "Personal Protective and Lifesaving Equipment, eye and face protection (construction)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 9, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 25, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Eye and Face Protection, 29 CFR 1926.102, requires employers to ensure that employees wear the right eye and face protection whenever work exposes them to flying particles, molten metal, chemicals, gases, vapors or harmful light. This course covers the ANSI Z87.1 marking requirement, the main types of protection and when each is used, prescription and over-the-glasses options, hazard assessment, care and replacement, and the public works tasks that most often lead to eye injuries and citations.", "objectives": ["State the employer duty under 1926.102 and the six hazard categories that trigger it.", "Identify ANSI Z87.1 compliant eye and face protection by its markings.", "Match safety glasses, goggles, face shields, welding protection and arc rated shields to the hazards they control.", "Explain why a face shield is worn over safety glasses or goggles and never alone.", "Select the correct welding filter shade and the right option for employees who wear prescription lenses.", "Inspect, clean and replace eye and face protection and recognize the conditions most often cited."], "sections": [{"heading": "The employer duty and the hazards that trigger it", "html": "<p>Under 29 CFR 1926.102 the employer must <strong>ensure</strong> that each employee uses appropriate eye or face protection when exposed to hazards from:<br />\n- <strong>Flying particles</strong> from grinding, chipping, cutting, drilling and sawing<br />\n- <strong>Molten metal</strong> from welding, brazing and torch cutting<br />\n- <strong>Liquid chemicals</strong>, including splashes when pouring, mixing or spraying<br />\n- <strong>Acids or caustic liquids</strong> such as battery acid and sodium hydroxide<br />\n- <strong>Chemical gases or vapors</strong> that irritate or burn the eyes<br />\n- <strong>Potentially injurious light radiation</strong> from welding arcs, cutting torches, lasers and ultraviolet lamps</p>\n<p>Ensure is the key word. It is not enough to hand out safety glasses or post a sign. Supervisors must make sure protection is worn whenever the hazard is present. The general industry rule at 1910.133 says the same thing in nearly the same words, so the duty follows you whether the work is classified as construction or maintenance.</p>\n<p>Most eye injuries come from small, fast particles that strike before the worker can blink, and many happen to people standing near the work rather than doing it. The protection zone is the hazard area, not just the person holding the tool.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Eye and face protection stays on OSHA's most cited list because the violation is easy to see. An inspector walking a jobsite or a shop does not need a monitor or a records review. If someone is <strong>grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection</strong>, the citation writes itself.</p>\n<p>The most common findings are:<br />\n- Employees grinding, cutting, chipping or drilling with no eye protection at all<br />\n- Employees wearing ordinary prescription glasses or sunglasses instead of safety eyewear<br />\n- <strong>Face shields used without safety glasses or goggles underneath</strong><br />\n- Side shields missing from safety glasses<br />\n- Welding helpers and bystanders exposed to arc flash with no filter lenses<br />\n- Eyewear so scratched or fogged that workers push it up on their heads</p>\n<p>Every one of these is a supervision and habit problem more than an equipment problem. The equipment is cheap and usually in the truck.</p>"}, {"heading": "The ANSI Z87.1 marking", "html": "<p>Protective eye and face devices must comply with the <strong>ANSI/ISEA Z87.1</strong> consensus standard. OSHA incorporates specific editions of Z87.1 by reference in 1926.102 and 1910.133 and also accepts devices that the employer can show are at least as protective. In practice, look for the <strong>Z87</strong> marking molded or stamped on the frame and lens.</p>\n<p>Common markings:<br />\n- <strong>Z87</strong> means the device meets the basic impact requirements.<br />\n- <strong>Z87+</strong> means the device passed the <strong>high impact</strong> tests, which is what you want for grinding, chipping, chainsaw and chipper work.<br />\n- <strong>D3</strong> indicates splash and droplet protection, <strong>D4</strong> dust and <strong>D5</strong> fine dust.<br />\n- <strong>W</strong> followed by a number is a welding filter shade. <strong>U</strong> followed by a number is an ultraviolet filter.<br />\n- <strong>Z87-2</strong> on prescription frames means the frame meets the standard.</p>\n<p>Ordinary eyeglasses, reading glasses and sunglasses are not safety eyewear even if the lenses are polycarbonate. They do not carry the marking, lack side protection, and can shatter into the eye on impact. If it does not say Z87, it does not count.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of protection and what each is for", "html": "<p><strong>Safety glasses with side shields</strong> are the baseline for flying particle hazards: grinding, drilling, sawing, mowing, trimming, hammering and most shop work. Side shields are required when particles can come from the side, which is nearly always. Wraparound frames provide them built in.</p>\n<p><strong>Goggles</strong> seal around the eyes. <strong>Impact goggles</strong> with direct vents are for heavy particle work. <strong>Chemical splash goggles</strong> with indirect vents or no vents are for pouring, mixing and transferring liquids, and for dusty work. Goggles fit over most prescription glasses.</p>\n<p><strong>Face shields</strong> protect the whole face from large particles, splash and spray, but they are <strong>secondary protection</strong>. A face shield is always worn <strong>over safety glasses or goggles</strong>, never alone, because particles and liquid can get around and under the shield. Use them for grinding, chipping, chainsaw and chipper work, pressure washing and chemical transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Welding helmets and goggles</strong> with filter lenses protect against arc flash, sparks and molten metal. The lens shade must match the process. OSHA gives minimum shades in Table E-1 of 1926.102: roughly shade 10 to 12 for common arc welding and shade 3 to 6 for gas welding and oxygen cutting. Start darker and go lighter until you can see the work. A welding helmet is worn over safety glasses.</p>\n<p><strong>Laser protection</strong> must be rated for the specific wavelength and power of the laser in use, as marked on the eyewear.</p>\n<p><strong>Arc rated face shields and balaclavas</strong> protect against the heat of an electrical arc flash. They come from the electrical safety rules rather than 1926.102, but they are worn with safety glasses underneath for the same reason.</p>"}, {"heading": "Prescription lenses and fit", "html": "<p>Employees who wear corrective lenses have three compliant choices:<br />\n- <strong>Prescription safety glasses</strong> with Z87-2 marked frames, impact rated lenses and side shields. This is the most comfortable option for daily wear and many employers pay for a pair through a safety eyewear program.<br />\n- <strong>Over-the-glasses (OTG) safety glasses</strong> or goggles designed to fit over regular eyeglasses without disturbing them. These work well for visitors, occasional tasks and employees waiting on a prescription pair.<br />\n- <strong>Safety goggles or a full face respirator</strong> that fit over regular glasses, where the task also calls for a seal.</p>\n<p>Contact lenses are not protection on their own, and employees should tell their supervisor they wear them so first aid responders know.</p>\n<p>Fit matters for every type. Eyewear that slides down, pinches or fogs will end up on top of someone's head. Try different frame sizes, use anti-fog lenses, add a retention strap for overhead work, and make sure goggles seal without gaps.</p>"}, {"heading": "Hazard assessment, care and replacement", "html": "<p>The PPE rules require a <strong>hazard assessment</strong> of each task to decide what protection is needed. Walk through the job and ask: Can anything fly, splash, spray or arc toward the face? From which direction? Can it reach anyone nearby? Then choose protection that fully controls the hazard, combining devices when one hazard calls for a seal and another calls for face coverage.</p>\n<p>Good eyewear care:<br />\n- <strong>Clean</strong> lenses daily with water or lens cleaner and a soft cloth. Dry wiping grinds dust into the lens.<br />\n- <strong>Inspect</strong> before each use for cracks, deep scratches, loose or missing side shields, stretched straps and pitted welding lenses. Pits and scratches weaken the lens and scatter light.<br />\n- <strong>Replace</strong> eyewear that is cracked, badly scratched or fogged beyond cleaning, and replace any device that has taken a hard hit even if damage is not visible.<br />\n- <strong>Store</strong> eyewear in a case or clean pouch, not loose in a toolbox or on the dash.<br />\n- <strong>Disinfect</strong> shared face shields and goggles between users.</p>\n<p>Keep spare safety glasses in every truck and shop so a scratched pair is never a reason to skip protection.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Public Services.</strong> Fleet and sign shop work is where most eye injuries happen: bench and angle <strong>grinding</strong>, cutoff wheels, wire brushing, chiseling and blowing off parts with compressed air. Z87+ safety glasses are the minimum, with a face shield added for grinding and cutoff work. Welders need the right filter shade, and helpers need protection against arc flash and chipping slag. <strong>Forestry</strong> crews running chainsaws and chippers need high impact safety glasses plus a face screen on the helmet, because chips fly fast and from all directions. Streets crews <strong>cutting concrete and asphalt</strong> need Z87+ glasses and a face shield when the saw throws slurry. <strong>Pressure washing</strong> throws grit back at the face, so wear glasses and a shield. Battery service means <strong>acid</strong>, so goggles and a face shield when checking, filling or jump starting.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Services.</strong> Water and wastewater operators handling <strong>chlorine, sodium hypochlorite, caustic soda and ferric chloride</strong> need chemical splash goggles, and a face shield when pouring or connecting lines. Know where the eyewash is before you open the valve. Collections and solid waste crews face splash from sewage and hydraulic lines, and grinding or cutting pipe in the field.</p>\n<p><strong>Electric Services.</strong> Line crews and substation technicians need safety glasses for every task because of flying debris and hardware overhead. Energized work above the arc flash threshold requires an <strong>arc rated face shield and balaclava</strong> or arc flash hood, with safety glasses underneath. Substation battery banks call for goggles and a face shield during maintenance. Meter technicians need safety glasses whenever a meter socket is opened.</p>\n<p>The rule is the same in every division: if a task can send anything toward your face, protection is on before the work starts, and face shields always go over glasses.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["The employer must ensure eye and face protection is used whenever employees are exposed to flying particles, molten metal, liquid chemicals, acids or caustics, chemical gases or vapors, or harmful light radiation.", "Only devices marked Z87 under ANSI Z87.1 count as protective eyewear, and Z87+ means high impact rated.", "Safety glasses with side shields are the baseline, goggles seal out splash and dust, and face shields add coverage for the whole face.", "A face shield is secondary protection and is always worn over safety glasses or goggles, never alone.", "Welding filter shades must match the process, starting from the minimums in Table E-1 of 1926.102.", "Employees who need corrective lenses must use prescription safety glasses or over-the-glasses protection, because ordinary eyeglasses are not safety eyewear.", "Inspect eyewear before each use and replace anything cracked, deeply scratched or struck hard.", "The most common citations are employees grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection and face shields worn without glasses underneath."], "questions": [{"text": "A mechanic is using an angle grinder with a cutoff wheel. What is the correct eye and face protection?", "options": ["A face shield alone, because it covers the whole face", "Ordinary prescription glasses with a face shield", "Z87+ safety glasses with side shields, plus a face shield over them", "Chemical splash goggles alone"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Grinding and cutoff work produces high speed particles, so high impact safety glasses are the primary protection and a face shield is added over them for face coverage. A face shield is never worn alone."}, {"text": "What does the marking Z87+ on a pair of safety glasses mean?", "options": ["The lenses are prescription", "The device passed the high impact tests of ANSI Z87.1", "The lenses filter welding radiation", "The glasses are approved for laser work"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Z87+ marks eyewear that passed the high impact requirements of ANSI Z87.1, which is what grinding, chipping and chainsaw work call for. Plain Z87 means basic impact only."}, {"text": "Which of these is NOT one of the hazards listed in 1926.102 that requires eye or face protection?", "options": ["Flying particles", "Liquid chemicals", "Potentially injurious light radiation", "Loud noise"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standard lists flying particles, molten metal, liquid chemicals, acids or caustic liquids, chemical gases or vapors, and injurious light radiation. Noise is covered by the hearing conservation rules, not eye and face protection."}, {"text": "Why must a face shield be worn over safety glasses or goggles rather than by itself?", "options": ["Particles and liquids can travel around and under the shield and reach the eyes", "Face shields are not rated for any impact", "The standard only recognizes face shields for welding", "Face shields fog too quickly to be used alone"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A face shield is secondary protection. Its open bottom and sides let particles, dust and splash reach the eyes, so primary eye protection must be worn underneath."}, {"text": "An employee who wears prescription eyeglasses needs to run a chipper for the afternoon. Which option complies with the standard?", "options": ["Wearing the prescription glasses, since they are polycarbonate", "Wearing the prescription glasses with the chipper face screen down", "Wearing over-the-glasses safety glasses or goggles with the face screen, or Z87-2 prescription safety glasses", "Removing the glasses and working without them"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Ordinary eyeglasses are not safety eyewear, and a face screen alone is secondary protection. The employee needs prescription safety glasses or over-the-glasses protection under the screen."}, {"text": "A water plant operator is connecting a hose to a tote of sodium hypochlorite. What is the right protection?", "options": ["Safety glasses with side shields only", "Chemical splash goggles, with a face shield added for pouring or connecting lines", "A welding helmet with a shade 5 lens", "A dust mask and sunglasses"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Corrosive liquids call for splash goggles that seal around the eyes, and a face shield over them when the task can spray or splash. Safety glasses alone leave gaps for liquid to enter."}, {"text": "Where does OSHA list the minimum filter lens shade numbers for welding and cutting?", "options": ["Section 8 of the Safety Data Sheet", "The NIOSH respirator certification", "Appendix D of 1910.134", "Table E-1 of 1926.102"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Table E-1 in 1926.102 gives the minimum protective shade for each welding and cutting operation. Welders start with the minimum or darker and adjust lighter until they can see the work."}, {"text": "When should safety glasses be replaced?", "options": ["Only when the lenses crack completely", "Once every five years regardless of condition", "When they are cracked, deeply scratched, fogged beyond cleaning, or have taken a hard impact", "Only when a supervisor notices damage"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Scratches and pits weaken the lens and scatter light, and an impact can damage a lens without visible signs, so eyewear is inspected before each use and replaced when its condition is in doubt."}, {"text": "A line crew is about to perform energized work above the arc flash threshold. What face protection is required?", "options": ["An arc rated face shield or arc flash hood worn over safety glasses", "A standard polycarbonate face shield worn alone", "Chemical splash goggles", "A welding helmet with a shade 10 lens"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Arc flash exposure requires an arc rated face shield with balaclava or an arc flash hood, with safety glasses underneath. Standard face shields can melt and are not arc rated."}, {"text": "Which situation is the most common reason employers are cited under the eye and face protection standard?", "options": ["Safety glasses stored in a toolbox", "Employees observed grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection", "Eyewash stations more than 10 seconds away", "Welding lenses one shade lighter than Table E-1"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The citation inspectors write most often is for employees seen grinding, chipping or cutting without eye protection, followed by face shields worn without safety glasses underneath. Both are visible on a short walk through a shop or jobsite."}]}, {"slug": "machine-guarding", "title": "Machine Guarding", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "standard_title": "Machine Guarding, general requirements (general industry)", "category": "osha_top10", "top10_rank": 10, "divisions": ["electric", "environmental", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "Machine guarding keeps hands, fingers, hair and clothing out of the moving parts of shop, plant and field equipment. This course covers the general guarding requirement in 29 CFR 1910.212, the types of guards and safeguarding devices, the special rules for fans and abrasive wheel grinders, and the habits that keep guards in place. Examples come from fleet and sign shops, treatment plants, transfer stations and tree and mowing crews.", "objectives": ["Identify the four main mechanical hazards: the point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks.", "Explain why a guard must keep every part of the body out of the danger zone during the operating cycle.", "Distinguish fixed, interlocked, adjustable and self-adjusting guards from safeguarding devices such as presence sensing and two-hand controls.", "Apply the specific rules for fan blade guards, anchoring fixed machinery and bench grinder work rests and tongue guards.", "Recognize common guarding failures on public works equipment and know what to do when a guard is missing or damaged."], "sections": [{"heading": "The general requirement", "html": "<p>29 CFR 1910.212 is short, but it applies to almost every machine in a public works shop or plant. It says that <strong>one or more methods of machine guarding</strong> must be provided to protect the operator and other employees in the area from hazards such as those created by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>point of operation</strong>, where the machine actually cuts, shapes, bores or forms the material,</li>\n<li><strong>ingoing nip points</strong>, where a belt meets a pulley, a chain meets a sprocket, or two rollers turn toward each other,</li>\n<li><strong>rotating parts</strong> such as shafts, couplings, flywheels, chucks and fan blades, and</li>\n<li><strong>flying chips and sparks</strong> thrown off by grinding, cutting and drilling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The guard must do its job without creating a new hazard. A guard with a sharp edge or a pinch point of its own does not meet the standard.</p>\n<p>The word \"machine\" covers more than big production equipment. A bench grinder, a drill press, a pedestal fan, a pump coupling and the belt drive on a blower are all machines with parts that can injure.</p>"}, {"heading": "Why this standard is on the Top 10", "html": "<p>Machine guarding sits on OSHA's most frequently cited list every year, and the citations follow a predictable pattern:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Missing or removed guards.</strong> A guard was taken off for maintenance or because it was in the way and never went back on. Belt and coupling guards leaning against the wall next to the machine are a classic finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Unguarded bench grinders.</strong> Work rests set too far from the wheel, tongue guards missing or opened too far, and side guards removed are common items on any shop inspection.</li>\n<li><strong>Exposed belts and pulleys.</strong> Blowers, compressors, conveyors and shop equipment with open belt drives within reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Point of operation exposure.</strong> Shears, presses and saws where fingers can reach the cutting or forming zone during the cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Guards that are too open or too short.</strong> Covers that leave gaps big enough for a hand or that stop short of the nip point.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public works shops are small and busy, and equipment is often older. That is where guards go missing and stay missing.</p>"}, {"heading": "The point of operation and the danger zone", "html": "<p>The <strong>point of operation</strong> is the area on a machine where work is actually performed on the material. On a drill press it is where the bit enters the stock. On a metal shear it is the blade. On a band saw it is the exposed blade above the table.</p>\n<p>The standard sets a simple test for point of operation guarding: the guard or device must <strong>prevent the operator from having any part of the body in the danger zone during the operating cycle</strong>. If a finger, hand or arm can reach the hazard while the machine can move, the guarding is not adequate.</p>\n<p>Special hand tools for placing and removing material may be used, but they are an addition to guarding, not a substitute for it.</p>\n<p>The standard names several machines that usually need point of operation guarding, including guillotine cutters, shears, power presses, milling machines, power saws, jointers and forming rolls. Sign shops and fleet shops have several of these.</p>"}, {"heading": "Types of guards and safeguarding devices", "html": "<p><strong>Guards</strong> are physical barriers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed guards</strong> are permanent parts of the machine, such as a bolted belt cover or a coupling guard. They are the simplest and most reliable, and they are preferred whenever the operator does not need access during operation.</li>\n<li><strong>Interlocked guards</strong> shut the machine off when the guard is opened. The machine cannot restart until the guard is back in place. Many balers and chippers use interlocks on access doors.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjustable guards</strong> can be positioned to fit different sizes of stock, such as a band saw blade guard lowered to just above the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-adjusting guards</strong> move out of the way as the stock is fed and return when it is removed, like the lower blade guard on a circular saw.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Devices</strong> control access another way.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Presence sensing devices</strong> use light curtains, laser scanners or pressure mats to stop the machine when a person enters the zone.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-hand controls</strong> require both hands on buttons away from the point of operation during the hazardous part of the cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Pullbacks</strong> attach to the operator's wrists and pull the hands away as the machine cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Restraints</strong> tie the hands back so they cannot reach the danger zone at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whenever possible, a guard must be <strong>affixed to the machine</strong>. If that is not possible, it may be secured elsewhere, but it still must stay in place during operation.</p>"}, {"heading": "Fans, anchoring and bench grinders", "html": "<p>Three specific rules come up constantly in public works inspections.</p>\n<p><strong>Fan blades.</strong> When the periphery of a fan blade is less than <strong>7 feet above the floor or working level</strong>, the blades must be guarded, and the openings in the guard may be no larger than <strong>one half inch</strong>. Pedestal fans in shops and air movers in plants fall under this rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Anchoring fixed machinery.</strong> Machines designed for a fixed location must be <strong>securely anchored</strong> so they cannot walk or tip. A pedestal grinder that wobbles under load is a citation and a hazard.</p>\n<p><strong>Abrasive wheel grinders.</strong> 29 CFR 1910.215 adds specific rules for bench and pedestal grinders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>work rest</strong> must be kept adjusted to within <strong>one eighth inch</strong> of the wheel so the work cannot be pulled into the gap and jammed against the wheel.</li>\n<li>The <strong>tongue guard</strong> (the adjustable spark guard at the top of the wheel housing) must be kept within <strong>one quarter inch</strong> of the wheel.</li>\n<li>The side guards must cover the spindle, nut and flange, leaving only the working portion of the wheel exposed.</li>\n<li>Every new wheel gets a <strong>ring test</strong> before mounting. Tap the wheel lightly with a nonmetallic tool. A sound wheel rings clearly; a cracked wheel sounds dull and must be scrapped.</li>\n<li>Wheels must be rated for the grinder's speed. Stand to the side and let a new wheel run for a minute before use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As the wheel wears down, the gaps grow. Readjust the work rest and tongue guard with the grinder off and unplugged.</p>"}, {"heading": "Keep guards on, and what to do when they are not", "html": "<p>Guarding only works when it is in place. These rules apply to everyone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Never remove or bypass a guard while the machine is running.</strong> If a guard must come off for maintenance, the machine is locked out first and the guard goes back on before the lockout is released.</li>\n<li><strong>Never tie back, block or defeat an interlock.</strong> A taped-down interlock switch turns an interlocked guard into no guard at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect before you start.</strong> Check that the guard is present, secure and not cracked, bent or missing fasteners.</li>\n<li><strong>Report and tag.</strong> A machine with a missing or damaged guard is tagged out of service and reported to a supervisor. Do not use it until the guard is repaired or replaced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Operator training</strong> is part of guarding. Before using a machine, you should know its hazards, how the guards protect you, how to check that they are working and what to do if they are not. Loose clothing, gloves near rotating parts, long hair and jewelry add risk around any rotating machinery and should be controlled before the machine is started.</p>\n<p>Replacing a guard is cheap. A finger is not.</p>"}, {"heading": "What this means for Public Works crews", "html": "<p><strong>Fleet and sign shops.</strong> Bench grinders need the work rest and tongue guard adjusted every time the wheel wears. Drill presses need chuck and belt guards in place and stock clamped so it cannot spin. Band saws need the blade guard set just above the work. Metal shears need point of operation guarding or devices that keep fingers out of the blade. Tire machines have bead breakers and turntables that can crush and pinch, so hands stay clear while the machine moves.</p>\n<p><strong>Water, wastewater and solid waste.</strong> Pump couplings, belt and chain drives on blowers and conveyors, and clarifier drives must have fixed guards that cover the nip points and the rotating shaft. Guards removed during a rebuild go back on before the equipment returns to service. Balers and compactors at transfer stations rely on interlocked access doors and point of operation guarding, and no one reaches into the chamber or clears a jam while the machine has power.</p>\n<p><strong>Streets, forestry and electric field crews.</strong> Chipper infeed chutes, feed control bars and discharge guards must be in place and working, and operators stay at the side of the infeed with no loose clothing. Mower decks need discharge chutes and belt covers, and the PTO shaft on tractors and trailed equipment needs its spinning shield intact. Portable generators, compressors and line truck hydraulic pumps have belts, fans and couplings that need the same attention as shop equipment.</p>\n<p>Across all three divisions, the simplest habit is the most effective: if the guard is not on, the machine does not run.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["Every machine needs one or more guarding methods that protect the operator and nearby employees from the point of operation, nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks.", "Point of operation guarding must keep every part of the body out of the danger zone during the operating cycle.", "Fixed guards are preferred, guards should be attached to the machine where possible, and no guard may create a hazard of its own.", "Fan blades within 7 feet of the floor must be guarded with openings no larger than one half inch, and fixed machines must be anchored.", "On bench grinders, keep the work rest within one eighth inch and the tongue guard within one quarter inch of the wheel, and ring test every new wheel.", "Guards are never removed or bypassed while a machine is running, and a machine with a missing or damaged guard is tagged out until it is fixed.", "The most common citations are missing or removed guards, unguarded bench grinders and exposed belts and pulleys."], "questions": [{"text": "Which of the following is one of the hazards that 29 CFR 1910.212 says machine guarding must protect against?", "options": ["Noise above 85 decibels", "Ingoing nip points", "Poor lighting at the workstation", "Slippery floors around the machine"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The standard names the point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks as the hazards guarding must address. Noise, lighting and floors are covered by other standards."}, {"text": "What is the test for whether point of operation guarding is adequate?", "options": ["The guard is painted a warning color", "The guard slows the operator's hands enough to react", "The guard prevents any part of the operator's body from being in the danger zone during the operating cycle", "The operator has signed a form acknowledging the hazard"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The standard requires that the guarding device keep every part of the body out of the danger zone while the machine cycles. If a hand can reach the hazard, the guard does not meet the rule."}, {"text": "A baler door shuts the machine off when it is opened and will not let it restart until the door is closed. What type of guard is this?", "options": ["Interlocked guard", "Self-adjusting guard", "Adjustable guard", "Fixed guard"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An interlocked guard cuts power or stops the machine when the guard is opened and prevents restart until it is back in place."}, {"text": "A pedestal fan in the fleet shop has blades about 5 feet above the floor. What does the standard require?", "options": ["No guard is required because the fan is portable", "A guard with openings no larger than one inch", "A guard only if the fan is within 3 feet of a workstation", "A guard with openings no larger than one half inch"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "When the fan blade periphery is less than 7 feet above the floor or working level, the blades must be guarded and the guard openings may be no larger than one half inch."}, {"text": "How close must the work rest on a bench grinder be kept to the wheel?", "options": ["Within one half inch", "Within one quarter inch", "Within one eighth inch", "Within one inch"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "1910.215 requires the work rest to be adjusted to within one eighth inch of the wheel so work cannot be pulled into the gap. The tongue guard is the component kept within one quarter inch."}, {"text": "Before mounting a new grinding wheel, what should you do?", "options": ["Soak the wheel in water to cool it", "Tap it lightly with a nonmetallic tool and listen for a clear ring", "Run it at double speed for one minute to test strength", "File the edges smooth to remove burrs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The ring test checks for cracks. A sound wheel rings clearly when tapped; a dull sound means the wheel is cracked and must be discarded."}, {"text": "A guard must be removed to replace a belt on a blower. What is the correct sequence?", "options": ["Remove the guard while the blower is running so you can see the belt track, then lock out", "Lock out the blower, remove the guard, replace the belt, reinstall the guard, then release the lockout", "Lock out the blower, replace the belt and reinstall the guard at the next scheduled shutdown", "Have a second person stand by the stop button while the guard is off and the blower runs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Guards are never removed while a machine is running. The machine is locked out first, and the guard goes back on before the lockout is released and the machine returns to service."}, {"text": "Which of these is a safeguarding device rather than a guard?", "options": ["A bolted coupling cover on a pump", "A hinged belt cover with a spring latch", "A fixed mesh enclosure around a conveyor drive", "A two-hand control that requires both hands on buttons during the press cycle"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Guards are physical barriers. Two-hand controls, presence sensing devices, pullbacks and restraints are devices that keep the operator's hands out of the danger zone without a barrier."}, {"text": "You notice the coupling guard on a wastewater plant pump is missing. What should you do?", "options": ["Tag the pump out of service and report it to a supervisor before anyone uses it", "Operate the pump carefully and stay at least arm's length from the coupling", "Hang a caution sign and keep the pump running until the next maintenance day", "Cover the coupling with a rag so nothing gets caught"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A machine with a missing or damaged guard is taken out of service and reported. It should not run until the guard is repaired or replaced."}, {"text": "Which of these is among the most common machine guarding citations in shops like fleet and sign shops?", "options": ["Missing operator manuals", "Grinding wheels stored in a locked cabinet", "Exposed belts and pulleys on compressors and other equipment", "Guards painted a color other than yellow"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Exposed belts and pulleys, along with missing or removed guards and unguarded bench grinders, are the most frequent findings under the machine guarding standard."}]}, {"slug": "bucket-truck-rescue", "title": "Bucket Truck (Aerial Lift) Rescue", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.269 / 29 CFR 1910.67", "standard_title": "Aerial lifts and electric power work (rescue, first aid and CPR, emergency procedures)", "category": "rescue", "top10_rank": null, "divisions": ["electric", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "A worker who is shocked, injured or unresponsive in a bucket needs to be brought to the ground and given CPR within minutes, and the truck itself may be energized. This refresher covers the regulatory basis under 29 CFR 1910.269 and 1910.67, scene size up, de-energizing, using the lower controls, backup lowering methods, bucket to bucket rescue and removing the victim. It supports, but does not replace, hands on drills with the department's trucks, written procedures and manufacturer instructions.", "objectives": ["Identify the situations that require a bucket truck rescue and the OSHA paragraphs that require rescue, first aid and CPR training.", "Recognize the hazards of an energized truck, including step and touch potential, and explain when the truck must not be touched.", "Describe how to bring a bucket down with the lower controls and what to do when the lower controls fail.", "Explain how a second bucket truck and a rescue strap and rope are used to reach and move a victim.", "List the rescue sequence in order, from scene size up through CPR and AED use on the ground.", "Describe crew roles and the purpose of regular timed drills on the department's own trucks."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why rescue training is required", "html": "<p>Most overhead work is done from a bucket. That makes it the most likely place for a line worker to be hurt and a place a coworker cannot simply walk over to help. Situations that call for a rescue include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The operator is <strong>shocked</strong>, <strong>injured</strong>, <strong>unresponsive</strong> or has a <strong>medical emergency</strong> such as a heart attack or heat illness.</li>\n<li>The <strong>bucket or boom contacts an energized line</strong> and the truck itself may be energized.</li>\n<li><strong>Boom or hydraulic failure</strong> strands the bucket in the air.</li>\n<li>A <strong>truck fire</strong> starts below the operator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>OSHA ties rescue training to this work. <strong>1910.269(a)(2)</strong> requires training in the emergency procedures that apply to the job. <strong>1910.269(b)</strong> requires persons trained in <strong>first aid and CPR</strong> where employees work on or near exposed lines or equipment. <strong>1910.67</strong> sets the rules for aerial lifts, including <strong>lower controls that can override the upper controls</strong> and a <strong>body belt and lanyard attached to the boom or basket</strong> whenever a worker is in the bucket (1910.67(c)(2)). <strong>1926.453</strong> carries the same aerial lift requirements for construction work.</p>\n<p>This course is a <strong>refresher</strong>. It does not replace hands on drills with the department's own trucks, written procedures and manufacturer instructions, which govern where they differ.</p>"}, {"heading": "Scene size up", "html": "<p>Look before you touch anything. The hazards in a bucket truck rescue are the ones that create a second victim.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Energized lines</strong>. Is the bucket, boom or a conductor still in contact? Is the circuit still energized?</li>\n<li><strong>An energized truck</strong>. If the boom touched a line, the truck body, outriggers and anything bonded to it can be energized. Nothing may look different.</li>\n<li><strong>Step and touch potential</strong>. Current flowing from an energized truck into the earth creates a voltage gradient on the ground around it. Walking toward the truck, or touching it from the ground, can be fatal.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic</strong>. Bucket trucks work in the road. Close the lane or get a flagger.</li>\n<li><strong>Fire or smoke</strong> at the truck, the boom tip or the pole.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather and footing</strong>, especially at night or on ice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Say what you see out loud and assign roles before anyone moves.</p>"}, {"heading": "Calling for help and de-energizing", "html": "<p>One person calls <strong>911</strong> and <strong>dispatch</strong> and asks the <strong>system operator</strong> to de-energize and hold the circuit. Give the location, the number of victims, whether the truck may be energized and whether CPR is in progress.</p>\n<p>When contact is involved, de-energizing the circuit is the most important step. Until it is done:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Do not touch the truck, the outriggers or anything connected to it.</strong> Do not climb on it. Do not grab the lower controls.</li>\n<li>Keep everyone back. If you must move away from the truck, shuffle or hop with your feet together to limit step potential.</li>\n<li>Do not try to pull a conductor off the boom with your hands.</li>\n<li>If the department has a written procedure for approaching an energized vehicle, follow it exactly. If it does not, wait for confirmation that the circuit is dead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the circuit was never involved, such as a medical emergency, go directly to the lower controls.</p>\n<p>Rescuer PPE: <strong>rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class</strong>, <strong>hard hat</strong>, <strong>eye protection</strong> and <strong>fall protection</strong> for anyone entering a bucket. Put your gloves on before you reach for a control.</p>"}, {"heading": "First choice: lower the bucket from the ground", "html": "<p>The fastest and safest rescue is usually the one where nobody climbs. Every articulating or extensible boom lift built to carry people must have <strong>lower controls that can override the upper controls</strong>, and the rule against operating them without the occupant's permission has an exception for emergencies (1910.67(c)(2)). A rescue is exactly what they are for.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start the truck or engage the PTO if needed.</li>\n<li>Operate the lower controls to <strong>bring the bucket down and in</strong> to a safe landing spot, keeping the boom clear of the lines. Watch the boom tip, not the controls.</li>\n<li>Move smoothly. A jerky movement can throw a slumped victim against the edge or out of the bucket.</li>\n<li>Land the bucket where the ground crew can reach the victim, then release their fall protection and lift them out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Only when it is safe.</strong> If the truck or boom may still be energized, do not touch the lower controls or the truck until the line is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure has been followed.</p>"}, {"heading": "When the lower controls fail", "html": "<p>Hydraulic systems fail, engines stall and electrical contact can burn out control circuits. Every operator should know how to bring their own truck's boom down without the main system, following the <strong>manufacturer's procedure</strong> for that model. The common options are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emergency hydraulic pump</strong>, electric or 12 volt, that runs off the truck battery and powers the lower controls at reduced speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand pump</strong> at the pedestal or turret for trucks so equipped.</li>\n<li><strong>Auxiliary power</strong> from a second engine or a backup power unit.</li>\n<li><strong>Controlled bleed-down</strong> on trucks with a manual lowering valve, which lets the boom come down slowly under gravity. It works only on some units and only as the manual allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Practice these on the truck you drive. Find out where the hand pump handle is stored during a drill, not with a coworker unresponsive overhead.</p>\n<p>If none of these will bring the bucket down, or the truck cannot be touched, the next option is <strong>a second bucket truck</strong>. Position it where its boom can reach the victim while keeping clearance from the lines. Only a qualified operator in full PPE goes up.</p>"}, {"heading": "Bucket to bucket rescue and removing the victim", "html": "<p>When a second truck is used, the rescuer works from their own bucket.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bring the rescue bucket alongside or slightly below the victim's bucket so the victim can be moved across without a high lift over the rail.</li>\n<li>Attach a <strong>rescue strap and rope</strong> to the victim's harness D ring or body belt before releasing anything. The victim's own fall protection stays connected until the rescue strap is holding them.</li>\n<li>Work the victim across, keeping their head and airway clear. Secure them in the rescue bucket and bring both buckets down.</li>\n<li>If the victim must be lowered by rope instead, rig the rope to a rated point on the boom, never to the bucket liner or a rail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lifting a victim out of a bucket is hard. An unresponsive adult in a harness is dead weight in a tight space.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reposition the victim upright, facing you, with their arms over the rail if possible.</li>\n<li>Keep their fall protection connected <strong>until</strong> the rescue strap or the ground crew has them.</li>\n<li>Lift with the legs and a second rescuer where possible, then bring the victim over the rail to the ground or a tailgate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once the victim is down, <strong>start CPR</strong> if they are not breathing normally and attach the <strong>AED</strong> as soon as it arrives.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rescue sequence", "html": "<p>The department's written procedure and the truck manufacturer's instructions govern. The common order is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Size up the scene.</strong> Energized lines, energized truck, step and touch potential, traffic, fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Call for help.</strong> 911, dispatch, system operator to de-energize. Assign roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Put on PPE.</strong> Rubber gloves and sleeves, hard hat, eye protection, fall protection for anyone entering a bucket.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirm the truck is safe to touch.</strong> If contact occurred, wait for de-energization or follow the energized vehicle approach procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower the bucket with the lower controls</strong>, keeping the boom clear of the lines.</li>\n<li><strong>If the lower controls fail</strong>, use the emergency pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down per the manufacturer.</li>\n<li><strong>If the bucket still cannot be lowered</strong>, bring in a second bucket truck and do a bucket to bucket rescue with a rescue strap and rope.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove the victim</strong>, keeping their fall protection connected until they are secured.</li>\n<li><strong>Start CPR and attach the AED</strong> if the victim is not breathing normally. Continue until EMS takes over.</li>\n<li><strong>Brief EMS</strong> on the voltage, the contact and when it happened.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"heading": "Drills and the department procedure", "html": "<p>Crew roles should be decided before the emergency, not during it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two person crew</strong>: one calls for help and manages traffic, then goes to the lower controls or the second truck. The other prepares to receive the victim and start CPR.</li>\n<li><strong>Three person crew</strong>: add a person who controls traffic, meets EMS and stages the AED while the second runs the controls and the third receives the victim.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drill with the trucks you actually drive. Each model stores its emergency pump handle, bleed-down valve and auxiliary power switch in a different place, and one truck's procedure can damage another.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Run <strong>timed drills</strong> at least annually with a rescue mannequin in the bucket, including one where the lower controls are declared failed.</li>\n<li>Time from the alarm to CPR started. Four minutes is the goal.</li>\n<li>Practice bucket to bucket rescue when two trucks are available.</li>\n<li>After every drill, check that the rescue strap, rope and AED are on the truck and in good condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a refresher on the principles behind bucket truck rescue. <strong>The department's written procedure, the manufacturer's instructions for each truck, and hands on drills govern how the rescue is actually done.</strong> Where they differ from this course, follow them.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["1910.269(a)(2) requires emergency procedure training, 1910.269(b) requires first aid and CPR trained persons, and 1910.67 requires lower controls that override the upper controls and a body belt and lanyard in the bucket.", "If the boom or bucket contacted a line, the whole truck may be energized; do not touch it until the circuit is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure is followed.", "Step and touch potential around an energized truck can be fatal; keep people back and shuffle with feet together if you must move away.", "Call 911, dispatch and the system operator first, and get the circuit de-energized.", "The first choice is to bring the bucket down with the lower controls, smoothly, keeping the boom clear of the lines.", "If the lower controls fail, use the emergency pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down exactly as the manufacturer describes.", "Use a second bucket truck and a rescue strap and rope when the primary unit cannot be lowered, and keep the victim's fall protection connected until they are secured.", "Start CPR and attach the AED as soon as the victim is on the ground, and drill the whole sequence on your own trucks at least annually."], "questions": [{"text": "Which OSHA requirement says an aerial lift must have lower controls capable of overriding the upper controls?", "options": ["1910.67(c)(2)", "1910.269(b)", "1910.269(a)(2)", "1926.451(f)(6)"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1910.67(c)(2) requires lower controls that can override the upper controls and allows them to be used without the occupant's permission in an emergency. 1910.269(b) covers first aid and CPR and 1910.269(a)(2) covers emergency procedure training."}, {"text": "A bucket truck boom has contacted a primary conductor and the operator is slumped in the bucket. The truck looks normal. What should the ground crew do first?", "options": ["Run to the lower controls and bring the bucket down", "Pull the outrigger pads to break the truck's connection to the ground", "Stay back from the truck, call for the circuit to be de-energized, and treat the truck as energized until it is confirmed dead", "Have one person climb the boom to reach the operator"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After a line contact the truck body, outriggers and anything bonded to them may be energized even though nothing looks different. Nobody touches the truck until the circuit is de-energized or the department's energized vehicle approach procedure is followed."}, {"text": "What is step potential around an energized truck?", "options": ["The height of the step a rescuer must climb to reach the pedestal controls", "The voltage difference between a rescuer's two feet caused by current flowing from the truck into the ground", "The chance that the bucket will drop one step when the hydraulics fail", "The voltage carried on the truck's access steps only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Current flowing from an energized truck into the earth creates a voltage gradient on the ground. A person walking normally has one foot at a higher voltage than the other, and current flows through the body. Shuffling or hopping with feet together limits this."}, {"text": "Which action is the first choice for getting an unresponsive operator out of a bucket when the truck is confirmed safe to touch?", "options": ["Positioning a second bucket truck beside the first", "Lowering the victim by rope from the bucket", "Climbing the boom to reach the operator", "Bringing the bucket down with the lower controls at the truck"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The lower controls are the fastest and safest option because nobody has to leave the ground. The second truck and rope methods are used only when the bucket cannot be lowered."}, {"text": "The engine stalls during a rescue and the lower controls do nothing. What should the rescuer do next?", "options": ["Use the emergency hydraulic pump, hand pump, auxiliary power or controlled bleed-down according to the manufacturer's procedure for that truck", "Disconnect the hydraulic line at the pedestal so the boom can drop", "Rock the truck by moving the outriggers to shake the boom loose", "Wait for the fire department ladder truck"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Trucks are built with backup lowering methods for exactly this situation, and the manufacturer's procedure describes how each one works on that model. Improvised methods can drop the boom or injure the victim."}, {"text": "When a second bucket truck is used to reach a victim, when should the victim's own fall protection be disconnected from the first boom?", "options": ["As soon as the rescue bucket is within reach, so the victim can be pulled across quickly", "Before the rescue bucket arrives, to save time", "Only after a rescue strap and rope attached to the victim's harness or belt is holding them", "It should never be disconnected; the victim is lowered while still attached to the first boom"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The victim must always be attached to something that will hold them. The rescue strap and rope are attached and holding before the original fall protection is released."}, {"text": "Where should a rope be rigged if a victim must be lowered from a bucket by rope?", "options": ["To the fiberglass bucket liner", "To a rated attachment point on the boom", "To the bucket's top rail", "To the victim's tool bag strap"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The bucket liner and rail are not rated to carry a person's weight on a rope. A rated point on the boom is the only acceptable anchor."}, {"text": "Before reaching for the lower controls during a rescue, what PPE must the rescuer put on?", "options": ["Leather work gloves and a reflective vest", "A face shield only", "Hearing protection and safety toe boots", "Rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class, hard hat and eye protection"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The truck or controls may be energized even after the scene looks safe. Rubber gloves and sleeves rated for the voltage, a hard hat and eye protection are required, and anyone entering a bucket also needs fall protection."}, {"text": "In the rescue sequence, what is the correct order of these three steps?", "options": ["Lower the bucket, then call for help, then confirm the truck is safe to touch", "Confirm the truck is safe to touch, then call for help, then lower the bucket", "Call for help, then confirm the truck is safe to touch, then lower the bucket", "Lower the bucket, then confirm the truck is safe to touch, then call for help"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Help and de-energization are requested right after scene size up. The crew then confirms the truck is safe to touch, and only then operates the lower controls to bring the bucket down."}, {"text": "Why should bucket truck rescue drills be run on the department's own trucks rather than only in a classroom?", "options": ["Because OSHA requires every drill to be filmed from the bucket", "Because each truck model stores its emergency pump, bleed-down valve and auxiliary power in different places and uses a different procedure", "Because the classroom cannot simulate traffic noise", "Because the AED only works when connected to a truck battery"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Backup lowering methods differ from truck to truck, and using the wrong procedure can damage the unit or drop the boom. Timed drills on the actual trucks build the muscle memory the crew will need."}]}, {"slug": "pole-top-rescue", "title": "Pole Top Rescue", "standard": "29 CFR 1910.269", "standard_title": "Electric Power Generation, Transmission, and Distribution (rescue, first aid and CPR, emergency procedures)", "category": "rescue", "top10_rank": null, "divisions": ["electric", "public"], "duration_minutes": 30, "renewal_months": 12, "summary": "When a line worker is shocked or injured at the top of a pole, the crew on site has minutes to get them down and start CPR. This refresher covers the regulatory basis for rescue training under 29 CFR 1910.269, scene size up, clearing an energized contact, rescuer PPE, rigging and lowering, and crew roles. It supports, but does not replace, hands on timed drills using the department's written procedure and equipment.", "objectives": ["Explain why pole top rescue must be completed within about four minutes and where 1910.269 requires rescue, first aid and CPR training.", "Perform a scene size up that identifies energized conductors, downed lines, fire, traffic and pole condition before climbing.", "Describe how to clear an energized contact and what PPE a rescuer must wear.", "List the steps of a pole top rescue in order, from the call for help through CPR and AED use on the ground.", "Describe how the handline is rigged and how descent is controlled.", "Assign crew roles for two and three person crews and explain the purpose of regular timed drills."], "sections": [{"heading": "Why rescue training is required", "html": "<p>A line worker who is shocked, injured or unresponsive at the top of a pole cannot wait for the fire department. Electrical contact can stop the heart or breathing. <strong>Brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen</strong>, so the industry goal is to have the victim <strong>on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes</strong> of the incident. Only the crew on site can meet that clock.</p>\n<p>OSHA builds rescue into the electric power standard:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1910.269(a)(2)</strong> requires employees to be trained in the emergency procedures that apply to their work, and names <strong>pole top and manhole rescue</strong> as examples.</li>\n<li><strong>1910.269(b)</strong> requires persons trained in <strong>first aid and CPR</strong> where employees work on or near exposed lines or equipment, so a field crew can begin care immediately.</li>\n<li>Trained means practiced. Many utilities and public power systems <strong>drill pole top rescue at least annually</strong>, with a timer running.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a <strong>refresher</strong>. It does not replace hands on, timed drills with the department's written procedure and rescue equipment. Where this course and the department's written procedure differ, <strong>the department's procedure governs</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "Scene size up", "html": "<p>Rushing into a scene with an energized conductor turns one victim into two. Look before you climb.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Energized conductors</strong>. Is the victim still in contact with a line or equipment? Are the conductors still energized? Did anything burn down or fall?</li>\n<li><strong>Downed lines</strong> on the ground, on the truck, on a fence or in a tree. Assume they are energized.</li>\n<li><strong>Fire</strong> on the pole, in a transformer or in nearby brush.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic</strong>. Set cones or get a flagger.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather</strong>. Lightning, wind and ice change how you climb and rig.</li>\n<li><strong>Unstable pole</strong>. A pole that was struck, is burning or has a broken crossarm may not hold a second climber. Check it before you climb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Call out what you see so the whole crew hears it, then assign roles.</p>"}, {"heading": "Calling for help and clearing the contact", "html": "<p>Getting help moving is the first action, not the last. One person calls <strong>911</strong>, then <strong>dispatch</strong>, and asks the <strong>system operator</strong> to de-energize the circuit and hold it. Give the location, the number of victims and whether CPR is in progress. Keep the radio open.</p>\n<p><strong>Never touch a victim who is still in contact with an energized source.</strong> The current that injured them will pass through you. Break the contact first, in one of these ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Have the system operator <strong>de-energize the circuit</strong> and confirm it.</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>hot stick or other insulated tool</strong> rated for the voltage to move the conductor or the victim.</li>\n<li>Open a cutout or switch where that is within your training and the department procedure allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rescuer PPE is not optional because it is an emergency. Wear a <strong>hard hat</strong>, <strong>eye protection</strong>, <strong>rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper voltage class</strong> for the lines involved, and <strong>climbers with a body belt and fall protection</strong> as required by 1910.269(g)(2). If your rubber gloves are in the truck, get them. Thirty seconds is cheaper than a second victim.</p>"}, {"heading": "Reaching and assessing the victim", "html": "<p>Climb with your normal technique and your fall protection attached. Hurrying is how rescuers cut out and fall. Watch for the victim's tools and loose conductors as you go.</p>\n<p><strong>Position yourself above or beside the victim</strong>, depending on how they are hanging and what your department procedure calls for. Above lets you rig over a crossarm or through a rescue block and use your weight to control the line. Beside lets you reach the victim's belt. Either way, keep yourself and the handline clear of energized parts.</p>\n<p>Assess quickly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Responsiveness</strong>. Speak to the victim. Tap the shoulder.</li>\n<li><strong>Breathing</strong>. Look at the chest and listen for a few seconds. Do not spend time looking for a pulse at the top of a pole.</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding or burns</strong> that will matter during lowering. Lowering comes first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the victim is not breathing, your only job is to get them to the ground fast. Rescue breaths on the pole are not practical and delay what will actually save them.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rigging the handline", "html": "<p>The handline carries the victim's weight to the ground. Rig it once, rig it right.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pass the handline <strong>over a crossarm or through a rescue block</strong> above the victim, as close to directly over them as possible so they do not swing into the pole or a conductor.</li>\n<li>Attach the line to the victim using a <strong>rescue sling</strong> or the victim's own body belt D ring or strap, exactly as your department procedure specifies. Check the knot or clip by eye and by hand.</li>\n<li>Take up slack so the line holds most of the victim's weight <strong>before</strong> you release their fall protection. A victim released with slack in the line drops and may pull you off the pole.</li>\n<li>Keep the handline <strong>clear of energized parts</strong>. A rope laid across a hot phase becomes a conductor when it is wet.</li>\n<li>Make sure the line is long enough to reach the ground with rope to spare for the person controlling descent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only after the line is secure and holding do you cut or unbuckle the victim's belt or lanyard. Cut the strap, never the rope.</p>"}, {"heading": "Rescue sequence", "html": "<p>Every department writes its own version, and that written procedure governs. The common order is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Size up the scene.</strong> Energized lines, downed conductors, fire, traffic, pole condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Call for help.</strong> 911, dispatch, system operator to de-energize. Assign roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear the contact.</strong> De-energize or use insulated tools. Do not touch the victim until the source is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Put on PPE.</strong> Hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves, climbers, belt and fall protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Climb to the victim</strong> and position above or beside them, clear of energized parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess.</strong> Check responsiveness and breathing in a few seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Rig the handline</strong> over a crossarm or through a rescue block, attach it to the victim, take up slack.</li>\n<li><strong>Release the victim's fall protection</strong> once the line is holding.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower the victim.</strong> The rescuer controls descent from the pole, or a ground person takes the line, as the department procedure specifies. Lower smoothly and guide the victim away from the pole.</li>\n<li><strong>On the ground, start CPR and use the AED</strong> right away if the victim is not breathing normally. Continue until EMS takes over.</li>\n<li><strong>Descend and support</strong> the ground team. Tell EMS the voltage and the nature of the contact.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The target is step 10 within about four minutes.</p>"}, {"heading": "Crew roles, aerial lifts and the ground", "html": "<p>Rescue works best when nobody has to think about who does what.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two person crew</strong>: one climbs and rigs, one calls for help, manages traffic, handles the rope end if the procedure calls for it, and starts CPR the moment the victim touches down.</li>\n<li><strong>Three person crew</strong>: add a ground person who controls traffic, meets EMS and stages the AED while the second person takes the victim.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a <strong>bucket truck or aerial lift</strong> is on site and can reach the victim safely, it is often faster and easier on the victim than climbing. The same rules apply: clear the contact first, keep boom clearance from energized parts, and wear your rubber goods. A <strong>ladder</strong> can sometimes reach a victim on a short pole, but only when it can be set on firm ground, footed and clear of conductors. Do not waste time positioning a truck that cannot get to the pole. Climb.</p>\n<p>Once the victim is on the ground, <strong>start CPR</strong> if they are not breathing normally and attach the <strong>AED</strong> as soon as it arrives. Treat burns and bleeding after circulation is handled. Electrical contact victims can go into cardiac arrest minutes after they appear fine, so keep the AED on and someone watching until EMS takes over.</p>"}, {"heading": "Drills and the department procedure", "html": "<p>Reading about rescue does not make anyone ready to do it. The skills that matter under stress are the ones your hands know: finding the rescue block, tying the sling, controlling a loaded handline, cutting a strap one handed with gloves on.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drill <strong>at least annually</strong> with a <strong>rescue mannequin</strong> of realistic weight, in gloves and sleeves.</li>\n<li><strong>Time every drill</strong> from the alarm to the mannequin on the ground, and record it. Four minutes is the goal. Talk through what slowed you down.</li>\n<li>Rotate roles so every crew member has rigged and lowered.</li>\n<li>Drill the variations: victim hanging head down, victim on the far side of the crossarm, two person crew, night, rain.</li>\n<li>Inspect rescue equipment with the other climbing gear and keep it in the same place on every truck.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course is a refresher on the principles behind pole top rescue. <strong>The department's written procedure, the equipment on the truck, and hands on timed drills govern how you actually do it.</strong> If anything here differs from that procedure, follow the procedure and tell your supervisor.</p>"}], "key_takeaways": ["The goal is the victim on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes, because brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen.", "1910.269(a)(2) requires training in emergency procedures such as pole top rescue, and 1910.269(b) requires first aid and CPR trained persons on the crew.", "Size up the scene first: energized conductors, downed lines, fire, traffic, weather and the condition of the pole.", "Never touch a victim still in contact with an energized source; de-energize or clear the contact with insulated tools first.", "Wear hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves of the right class, climbers and fall protection before you climb.", "Rig the handline over a crossarm or through a rescue block, take up slack, and only then release the victim's fall protection.", "Start CPR and attach the AED as soon as the victim is on the ground, and keep the AED on until EMS takes over.", "The department's written procedure and timed drills with a rescue mannequin govern; this course is only a refresher."], "questions": [{"text": "Why does the electric utility industry set a goal of having a pole top victim on the ground with CPR started within about four minutes?", "options": ["Because the system operator can only hold a circuit out for four minutes", "Because brain injury begins within minutes when a person is not breathing or has no pulse", "Because most rescue handlines are only rated for four minutes of load", "Because EMS response time in most areas is four minutes"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Brain injury begins within minutes without oxygen, so the crew on site must get the victim down and start CPR fast. EMS and the system operator cannot act quickly enough to meet that clock."}, {"text": "Which paragraph of 1910.269 specifically names pole top and manhole rescue as examples of emergency procedures employees must be trained in?", "options": ["1910.269(a)(2)", "1910.269(b)", "1910.269(g)(2)", "1910.269(l)"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1910.269(a)(2) requires training in the emergency procedures that apply to an employee's work and gives pole top and manhole rescue as examples. Paragraph (b) covers first aid and CPR trained persons."}, {"text": "You arrive at a pole where a coworker is slumped in his belt and a primary conductor is lying across his shoulder. What should you do first?", "options": ["Climb immediately and pull the conductor off with your gloved hand", "Throw the handline over the crossarm so you can lower him as soon as you reach him", "Call for the circuit to be de-energized and clear the contact with an insulated tool before touching him", "Begin rescue breathing from the pole as soon as you reach him"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Never touch a victim who is still in contact with an energized source. The contact must be cleared by de-energizing the circuit or by using a hot stick or other properly rated insulated tool, or the rescuer becomes the second victim."}, {"text": "Which of these is the correct PPE for a rescuer climbing to a victim on a distribution pole?", "options": ["Hard hat and leather gloves, because speed matters more than rubber goods during a rescue", "Rubber gloves only, since sleeves slow down climbing", "Climbers and body belt without a fall protection lanyard, to save time", "Hard hat, eye protection, rubber gloves and sleeves of the proper class, climbers, belt and fall protection"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An emergency does not remove the hazards. The rescuer needs rubber gloves and sleeves rated for the voltage, a hard hat, eye protection, and climbers with a belt and fall protection as required by 1910.269(g)(2)."}, {"text": "When should the rescuer release the victim's fall protection from the pole?", "options": ["Before rigging the handline, so the victim can be repositioned", "Only after the handline is attached and holding most of the victim's weight", "As soon as the rescuer reaches the victim, to speed up the descent", "After the victim has been lowered a few feet to test the line"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The handline must be attached and the slack taken up so it is holding the victim before the victim's belt or lanyard is released. Releasing a victim with slack in the line causes a drop that can pull the rescuer off the pole."}, {"text": "Where should the handline be passed before it is attached to the victim?", "options": ["Over a crossarm or through a rescue block as close to directly above the victim as possible", "Around the victim's chest and then down to the ground person", "Over the nearest conductor, since it provides the highest anchor point", "Through the rescuer's own D ring so the rescuer can hold the victim's weight directly"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Rigging over a crossarm or through a rescue block above the victim lets the line carry the weight and keeps the victim from swinging into the pole or conductors. The line must stay clear of energized parts."}, {"text": "In a two person crew, what is the ground person's most important job once the victim starts coming down?", "options": ["Climbing the pole to help guide the victim past the crossarm", "Photographing the scene for the incident report", "Driving the truck closer so the bucket can be used", "Being ready to start CPR and attach the AED the moment the victim reaches the ground"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The point of the four minute goal is to start CPR as fast as possible. The ground person stages the AED, handles the rope if the procedure calls for it, and begins CPR immediately on touchdown."}, {"text": "A victim of electrical contact is lowered to the ground, is breathing, and says he feels fine. What should the crew do?", "options": ["Let him return to work once he can stand without help", "Have him sit in the truck and drive him home", "Keep the AED attached and someone watching him until EMS takes over, because cardiac arrest can occur minutes later", "Cancel the ambulance so EMS is available for other calls"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Electrical contact victims can go into cardiac arrest minutes after they appear fine. Keep the AED on, keep monitoring, and let EMS evaluate and transport."}, {"text": "When may a bucket truck be used instead of climbing to reach a pole top victim?", "options": ["Only when the victim is unresponsive", "When the truck is already on site and can reach the victim safely while keeping clearance from energized parts", "Whenever a truck is within ten minutes of the scene", "Never, because pole top rescue must be done by a climber"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An aerial lift that is already on site and can safely reach the victim is often faster and easier on the victim. If a truck cannot be positioned quickly, the rescuer should climb rather than lose time."}, {"text": "What is the purpose of running timed pole top rescue drills with a rescue mannequin at least annually?", "options": ["To satisfy the warranty terms for climbing equipment", "To replace the need for CPR and first aid certification", "To build the hands on skills and crew coordination needed to meet the four minute goal under stress", "To allow the department to skip the written rescue procedure"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Rescue skills are physical and fade without practice. Timed drills with a realistic weight in gloves and sleeves show whether the crew can meet the four minute goal and reveal what slows them down."}]}], "top10": [{"fiscal_year": 2026, "status": "preliminary", "period": "Oct. 1, 2025 to Aug. 31, 2026", "source": "https://www.osha.gov/top10citedstandards", "standards": [{"rank": 1, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "citations": 4041, "course_slug": "fall-protection-general"}, {"rank": 2, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "citations": 1961, "course_slug": "hazard-communication"}, {"rank": 3, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "citations": 1863, "course_slug": "lockout-tagout"}, {"rank": 4, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "title": "Scaffolding (construction)", "citations": 1725, "course_slug": "scaffolding"}, {"rank": 5, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "title": "Ladders (construction)", "citations": 1659, "course_slug": "ladders"}, {"rank": 6, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "citations": 1608, "course_slug": "respiratory-protection"}, {"rank": 7, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "citations": 1379, "course_slug": "powered-industrial-trucks"}, {"rank": 8, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "citations": 1273, "course_slug": "fall-protection-training"}, {"rank": 9, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "title": "Eye and Face Protection (construction)", "citations": 1120, "course_slug": "eye-face-protection"}, {"rank": 10, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "title": "Machine Guarding (general industry)", "citations": 1072, "course_slug": "machine-guarding"}]}, {"fiscal_year": 2025, "status": "final", "period": "Oct. 1, 2024 to Sept. 30, 2025", "source": "https://www.safetyandhealthmagazine.com (OSHA final FY 2025 data)", "standards": [{"rank": 1, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.501", "title": "Fall Protection, general requirements (construction)", "citations": 6992, "course_slug": "fall-protection-general"}, {"rank": 2, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.1200", "title": "Hazard Communication (general industry)", "citations": 3010, "course_slug": "hazard-communication"}, {"rank": 3, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.1053", "title": "Ladders (construction)", "citations": 2842, "course_slug": "ladders"}, {"rank": 4, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.147", "title": "Control of Hazardous Energy, lockout/tagout (general industry)", "citations": 2562, "course_slug": "lockout-tagout"}, {"rank": 5, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.134", "title": "Respiratory Protection (general industry)", "citations": 2294, "course_slug": "respiratory-protection"}, {"rank": 6, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.451", "title": "Scaffolding (construction)", "citations": 2286, "course_slug": "scaffolding"}, {"rank": 7, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.503", "title": "Fall Protection, training requirements (construction)", "citations": 2216, "course_slug": "fall-protection-training"}, {"rank": 8, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.178", "title": "Powered Industrial Trucks (general industry)", "citations": 2150, "course_slug": "powered-industrial-trucks"}, {"rank": 9, "standard": "29 CFR 1926.102", "title": "Eye and Face Protection (construction)", "citations": 1965, "course_slug": "eye-face-protection"}, {"rank": 10, "standard": "29 CFR 1910.212", "title": "Machine Guarding (general industry)", "citations": 1498, "course_slug": "machine-guarding"}]}], "pass_percent": 80, "credits": "Developed by Lorenzo McCoy and Mark Tompkins", "repo": "https://github.com/Tompkins52/Safety-Training"};
